                    :see-also [:apps :app :app-data :new-project]}
  :new-dataset {:theme :author-data
                :purpose "Declare a standalone dataset: `dataset create <name> --column n:type,… [--kind <kind>] [--display …] [--description …]`. A first-class `:datasets` entry — the raw landing/uploaded table a transform reads or writes. Stages through the install door like every schema mutation."
                :when "You need a table that exists BEFORE any transform writes it: an upload target, a landing table, the output a transform will declare."
                :patterns
                ["`--column` ACCUMULATES comma-joined, so `--column a:string,b:integer` and `--column a:string --column b:integer` are the same thing — twelve repeated flags are one list."
                 "`--kind` defaults to `:standalone`, which is the provenance axis (standalone / transform-output / object-backing), not a storage choice."]
                :anti-patterns
                [{:rule "Don't reach for `--kind materialization` here — this verb cannot express one."
                  :why "A materialization is ontology → dataset and must name the `:object-type` it materializes, while taking NO :columns. This verb has no --object-type flag and REFUSES an empty --column, so the two requirements cannot both be met. Author a materialization in the .ent."}]
                :examples
                ["dataset create stammblatt_dokumente --column dokument_id:string,bezeichnung:string --display \"Dokumente (Stammblätter)\""]
                :see-also [:datasets :new-transform :dataset-preview :upload]}
  :new-transform {:theme :author-data
                  :purpose "Scaffold ONE compute transform: `transform create <name> [--language sql|python|clojure] --input L:KIND:REF[:TABLE]… [--llm L:SRC:MODEL…] (--code … | --query … | --code-file P | --query-file P) <one output> [--incremental]`. Wires the shape, not the logic — the body defaults to a stub you fill with `transform edit`."
                  :when "Standing up a new pipeline step. Query FUNCTIONS (returning object-sets/values) are the `function` noun, not this one."
                  :patterns
                  ["The output is a THREE-WAY choice and exactly one may be given: `--output-type T --column n:t,… --pk f` (object rows) · `--output-dataset D` (a raw dataset) · `--output-link C --from-key c --to-key c` (link EDGES from 2-column SQL)."
                   "`--input` and `--llm` accumulate comma-joined, like every repeatable flag."]
                  :anti-patterns
                  [{:rule "Don't declare a second transform that produces rows for an object type another one already produces."
                    :why "An object type's rows have exactly one source of truth. Retire the one it replaces in the same push, or make this output `:role :supplement` if it adds COLUMNS rather than rows. `projection push <text> --preview` says so before the push."}]
                  :examples
                  ["transform create dokumente_vereinen --language python --input a:dataset:stammblatt_dokumente --output-type dokument --pk dokument-id"]
                  :see-also [:transforms :transform :edit :new-dataset :run-import]}
  :new-function {:theme :author-data
                 :purpose "One Function primitive: `function create <name> [--kind query|edit] …`. `query` (the default) takes the compute flags of `transform create` and lands in `:functions`; `edit` takes `--target-type T [--language …] [--source …] [--param n:t,…]` and is the write-back half."
                 :when "Compute that answers a QUESTION over live objects (query) or applies a written EDIT to them (edit) — as opposed to a batch transform that produces rows."
                 :patterns
                 ["`--param n:type` accumulates comma-joined and is what the caller must pass."]
                 :examples
                 ["function create offene-summe --kind query --input o:object-type:auftrag --query \"select …\""]
                 :see-also [:functions :function :new-transform :edit]}
  :new-object-type {:theme :author-schema
                    :purpose "Create an object type from flags: `ontology object-type create <name> --field n:type,… --plural … [--pk f] [--display …] [--symbol X] [--implements iface,…]`. Assembles the terse `:objects` fragment and stages it through the install door."
                    :when "A new writeback-hub type — one with no backing datasource, whose rows are created by actions rather than by a transform."
                    :patterns
                    ["The PK field (`--pk`, else the FIRST `--field`) becomes the type's `:title` — its identity/naming property."
                     "`--field` and `--implements` accumulate comma-joined."]
                    :anti-patterns
                    [{:rule "Don't try to express derived/rollup properties, parametric types or per-property options through flags."
                      :why "The flag form covers the common new-type shape only. Anything richer is authored as a .ent fragment — `ontology object-type edit <name> --from f.ent` is the door for that."}]
                    :examples
                    ["ontology object-type create werkzeug --field werkzeug-id:string,bezeichnung:string --display \"Werkzeug\" --plural \"Werkzeuge\" --symbol 🔧"]
                    :see-also [:describe :edit :new-link-type :new-interface :dependents]}
  :new-link-type {:theme :author-schema
                  :purpose "Create a link type with its backing: `ontology link-type create <name> --from A --to B [--cardinality …] [--display …]` plus `--fk <property> [--fk-side from|to]` (many-to-one, one-to-many, one-to-one: the foreign key on the many side) or `--join <dataset> --from-key <col> --to-key <col>` (many-to-many: the join table) — the typed edge between two object types."
                  :when "Two types need a navigable relationship. Nothing is created without a backing: a link would have nothing its edges come from."
                  :see-also [:describe :links :around :new-object-type]}
  :new-interface {:theme :author-schema
                  :purpose "Create an interface: `ontology interface create <name> --field n:type,… [--extends a,b] [--display …]` — a shared property list several object types can implement."
                  :when "The same properties recur across types and you want one name for them."
                  :anti-patterns [{:rule "Fix the NAME before you create one."
                                   :why "There is no `interface delete` — the surface carries create, list, show, rename and status only. A misnamed interface can be renamed or retired, never removed."}]
                  :see-also [:describe :new-object-type :new-shared-property]}
  :new-shared-property {:theme :author-schema
                        :purpose "Create a shared property: `ontology shared-property create <name> --type T [--display …]` — one property definition several types reuse."
                        :see-also [:describe :new-interface :new-value-type]}
  :new-value-type {:theme :author-schema
                   :purpose "Create a value type: `ontology value-type create <name> --type T [--one-of a,b] [--regex R] [--format f] [--min N] [--max N] [--display …]` — a base type plus the constraints that make it a domain."
                   :when "A property needs a narrower domain than its base type: an enum, a pattern, a range, a display format."
                   :patterns
                   ["`--type` is required; everything else is a constraint and they combine."]
                   :see-also [:describe :new-shared-property :new-object-type]}
  :protection {:theme :version
               :purpose "Protection PER RESOURCE: `protection <section/name>` protects it on main — it then changes only through a proposal; `--off` unprotects; bare lists what is protected. An unprotected resource lands directly. Only an owner of the resource's project may change it."
               :when "One resource carries something live and should be reviewed, while the rest of main stays directly writable — the solo default is: protect nothing."
               :examples ["protection" "protection object-types/werkzeug"
                          "protection object-types/werkzeug --off"]
               :see-also [:project-policy :protect :propose]}
  :project-use {:theme :project
                :purpose "WHERE new resources go when you name no project: `project use <p>` for this session (also a leading `--project <p>`), `project use <p> --default` for the whole instance (admin), bare shows both. A resource the derivation places (an action with its type, a link with its source) keeps that place; nothing that exists is moved. An instance with one project needs none of this."
                :when "You create several resources in one project and do not want to repeat --project on each."
                :examples ["project use lager" "project use haushalt --default" "project use"]
                :see-also [:project-policy :new-object-type]}
  :project-policy {:theme :project
                   :purpose "A project's OWN policy: `--protect-all` protects everything the project holds on main; `--review <edn>` adds review rules for its resources on top of the branch's (`{:when [...] :by [...] :count n :needs [:justification]}`); `--default-review` clears them. Bare reads. Only an owner of the project may change it."
                   :when "A team wants its resources reviewed its way — four eyes, a justification — without imposing that on every other project."
                   :examples ["project policy lager" "project policy lager --protect-all"
                              "project policy lager --review '[{:when [:always] :by [:other]}]'"
                              "project policy finanzen --review '[{:when [:breaking] :needs [:justification]}]'"]
                   :see-also [:protection :protect]}
  :access-request {:theme :operate
                   :purpose "ASK for access instead of editing grants: `access request role <project> --role <role>` or `access request group <group>`, for yourself or `--for <user>`, with an optional `--justification`. The owners of the project (or of the group type's project) decide; once approved the request applies itself — the grant is written, the membership linked."
                   :when "You need a role on a project or a place in a group and do not own it."
                   :examples
                   ["access request role lager --role ontology-editor --justification \"Inventur\"" "access request group nachtschicht --for eva"]
                   :see-also [:access-requests :access-decide :access-explain]}
  :access-requests {:theme :operate
                    :purpose "Open access requests; `--inbox` shows only those you may decide (you own the project they concern); `--status all|completed|rejected|closed`."
                    :examples ["access requests --inbox" "access requests --status all"]
                    :see-also [:access-request :access-decide]}
  :access-decide {:theme :operate
                  :purpose "Decide an access request: `access decide <id>` approves the tasks you may decide — when none stays open the request applies itself; `--reject` rejects and closes it. Verdicts are appended facts, like a proposal's."
                  :examples ["access decide 3f2a… --note \"ok für die Inventur\""
                             "access decide 3f2a… --reject"]
                  :see-also [:access-requests :access-request]}
  :access-explain {:theme :operate
                   :purpose "WHY a principal may or may not do an operation on a project: `access explain <user> <op> <project>`. The answer is the authority policy's own decision with the derivation it rests on — the grant, the group (also through groups in groups), the platform role or no restriction. Admin only."
                   :when "Someone is refused, or sees what they should not, and you need the reason rather than a guess."
                   :examples ["access explain alma update werkstatt"
                              "access explain ben manage-permissions lager"]
                   :see-also [:access :access-role]}
  :access-role {:theme :operate
                :purpose "Read or set a principal's PLATFORM role (`:rolle` on its user object): `access role <username|user-id> [--set <role>]`. Roles: consumer (end-user shell only), ontology-finder, ontology-viewer, ontology-editor, ontology-owner — the last four open the builder and grant their operations platform-wide; project rights come from `access grant`. Admin only; the change is audited (`access login-audit`) and holds from the next request — the user reloads the page, no new login."
                :when "A federated (LDAP/SSO) user, created as `consumer` by `ldap sync`, needs the builder — e.g. to review automation proposals."
                :patterns
                ["The least platform role that opens the builder is `ontology-finder`; give the project rights with `access grant`."]
                :examples ["access role alma" "access role alma --set ontology-finder"]
                :see-also [:access :grant :login-audit :ldap]}
  :register {:theme :operate
             :purpose "Provision a login: `access register <username> [--password …] [--user <id>] [--external user@REALM] [--auth-source <src>]`. Without `--password` a local user gets an auto-generated one."
             :when "Someone needs an account on this instance."
             :patterns
             ["`--external user@REALM` pre-provisions a FEDERATED (SSO) login instead: no local password, bound to the IdP identity, `--auth-source` defaulting to spnego."]
             :see-also [:access :grant :login-audit :sso]}
  :projects    {:theme :orient
                :purpose "List the ownership/permission containers (projects) on the branch."
                :see-also [:apps :grant]}
  :project-map {:theme :orient
                :purpose "ONE project's relational map in one call: its apps × the object-types they BIND, what the project OWNS (`owns`), the types bound by >1 app (candidate duplicate surfaces), and the link graph among them — how a project's apps actually connect."
                :when "The FIRST verb to run when you must understand how a project's apps relate to each other or spot metrics surfaced by several apps — instead of hand-assembling it from `apps` + `app` (per app) + `describe` (per type)."
                :patterns
                ["BOUND ≠ OWNED, and conflating them is the classic misread: `object-types` is what this project's apps READ (including types another project owns, reached across the seam); `owns` is what carries this project's `:project` RID and what its grants therefore cascade to. On prod, werft BINDS 16 and OWNS 5."
                 "`project-map unassigned` is the orphan report — the resources owned by NO project. It has no apps by definition, so read its `owns` block, not its (always empty) `apps`."
                 "Read the 'shared object-types' block first: a type bound by >1 app is where the same concept can be shown with divergent numbers."
                 "Follow the 'connections' link graph to see how the apps join through the ontology (and which links bridge OUT to another project's world)."]
                :examples ["project-map werft" "project-map unassigned" "project-map"
                           "project-map werft --json"]
                :see-also [:apps :app :overview :describe :projects :access]}
  :project-deps {:theme :orient
                 :purpose "The whole instance's project dependency graph: every reference that CROSSES a project boundary, measured from the schema, printed beside each project's declared `:imports` — plus the three ways the two disagree."
                 :when "Before splitting, merging or re-scoping projects, and whenever you are about to declare `:imports`: the measured half tells you what the declaration would have to say."
                 :patterns
                 ["The pair is COARSE INTENT vs FINE TRUTH. `:imports` is authored (a handful of names a human maintains); the edges are computed from the same ref registry the resolver walks. Never hand-maintain a per-resource dependency list — that is the list that rots."
                  "`NOT IMPORTED` fires only for a project that DECLARES `:imports` — a project that declares none has stated nothing to contradict, and shows as `undeclared`."
                  "The 'referenced from outside their owner' block IS the shared-core proposal: pull those resources into a project both sides may import and every crossing runs through it."
                  "An `unassigned` source row is a resource owned by no project at all — fix the ownership before reading the edge as a dependency."]
                 :examples ["project-deps" "project deps --json"]
                 :see-also [:projects :project-map :access :grant]}
  :show        {:theme :inspect :purpose "Display objects of a type (human table)."}
  :runs        {:theme :orient :purpose "List recent build / action / function runs."
                :see-also [:run :watch :run-import]}
  :run         {:theme :orient
                :purpose "Inspect ONE run by id — status + failure cause (error / failed transform / reasons); --diff adds the input/output diff."
                :when "A build/import failed and you need to know WHY — the detail `runs` (list) doesn't give. Don't use `show` for a run id."
                :examples ["run <run-id>" "run <run-id> --diff"]
                :see-also [:runs :watch :jobs]}
  :await       {:theme :orient
                :purpose "Wait for ONE handle a --detach returned — an eval job (`eval … --detach`) or a build run (`build run`) — until it ends: one line per change of state, exit 0 when it succeeded, ≠ 0 when it failed, timed out (--timeout-s, default 1800, at most 3600) or is UNKNOWN. An unknown handle fails at the first poll, so a wait on the wrong id ends in seconds instead of running silently into a timeout."
                :when "After every --detach, and as the whole command of a Monitor: `bin/sutra await <id>` instead of a hand-written poll loop over history or list verbs — those carry other ids (an eval JOB id never appears in `eval history`)."
                :anti-patterns [{:rule "Don't poll a list or history verb for the handle a start returned."
                                 :why "A start returns its own kind of handle (an eval job, not a run); filtering a list for it never matches and the loop stays silent until its timeout."}
                                {:rule "Don't call a STARTING verb in a loop to read its result."
                                 :why "`eval <suite>` and `build run` start a new run on every call; without a terminal a repeat within ten minutes is refused (--again overrides)."}]
                :examples ["await 4e5f129b-b0c2-41f4-988e-b7ef2e0f8a37"
                           "await <run-id> --timeout-s 600"]
                :see-also [:run :jobs :eval]}
  :jobs        {:theme :orient
                :purpose "The per-transform Job execution log of ONE build (run-id): each transform's status (WAITING/RUN_PENDING/RUNNING/COMPLETED/FAILED/ABORTED/DID_NOT_RUN), attempt, the commit its Job's transaction produced, and any error."
                :when "You have a run-id (from `runs`) and want the per-transform breakdown: which transforms COMPLETED, which FAILED and why, and what a retry would re-run. `run` gives the build's overall verdict; `jobs` decomposes it into its jobs."
                :examples ["jobs <run-id>"]
                :see-also [:run :runs :transactions :watch]}
  :health      {:theme :orient :purpose "Run all configured health checks."}
  :telemetry   {:theme :operate
                :purpose "What the deployment costs, measured by itself: per process (api, conductor, worker) its heap, RSS, swap, CPU, container cgroup memory and pg pool lanes (active/idle/waiting), the disks it writes; per egress agent its heap; the store's footprint — database size, memory settings, connections by client, relations by kind and the schemas each branch owns by branch state (purge-due, merged, closed, unowned …); and the findings that follow (swapping, near a limit, waiting for a pool, schemas a purge should have removed). Each process samples every `settings telemetry-ms` (30 s). Admin, read-only."
                :when "RAM, disk or CPU on the host look short, or a call sits at ~30 s: read what sutra itself holds before guessing at the topology. Not the host: what runs beside sutra (other containers, VMs) is not in it."
                :examples ["telemetry" "telemetry --json"]
                :see-also [:health :branches :trace]}
  :settings    {:theme :operate
                :purpose "The instance's operating policy, read and set at runtime — no deploy (admin settings such as resource queues): builds at once (one value for admission and worker), parallelism, retries and backoff, the source query timeout, every job's interval (0 stops it), run payload retention, request and app limits, the transforms' resource profiles. Bare: every setting by group with its value and where it comes from (stored · env · default). `settings <key>` one with its doc, default and fallback; `settings <key> <value>` sets it for every process (the others learn of it at once); `--reset` drops the stored value back to the environment variable or default. Topology, secrets and the security switches (SUTRA_ALLOW_ANON …) are NOT settings — the deploy fixes them. Admin."
                :when "Builds queue although nothing conflicts, a source query times out, a job ticks too often or too seldom, a transform needs a bigger profile — change it here instead of an env var and a deploy."
                :examples ["settings" "settings build-concurrency 6" "settings scheduler-ms 0"
                           "settings transform-profiles '{:xlarge {:wall-seconds 1800}}'"
                           "settings build-concurrency --reset"]
                :see-also [:telemetry :health]}
  :client-log  {:theme :operate
                :purpose "What the browsers reported: every page sends its render errors (the \"Dieser Bereich konnte nicht angezeigt werden\" box), uncaught errors, unhandled promise rejections, console errors, failed API calls and its navigation to the server, which appends them to the instance's client log. Newest first, with who, which URL, the browser, the message, and the stack mapped through the bundle's source map to cljs file:line. Bare = everything but navigation; `--errors` only what breaks a page; `--level a,b` names levels (incl. navigation); `--since 30m|2h|1d|<ISO>`, `--user`, `--q <text in message or url>`, `--limit` (50); `--stack` adds the raw stack and the whole mapped one. Admin, read-only."
                :when "A user reports an error on a page, a screenshot shows a render-error box, or a flow \"does nothing\" on a phone: read what the browser itself reported before reading code."
                :examples ["client-log --errors --since 30m" "client-log --q anna --stack"
                           "client-log --level navigation --user admin --limit 20"]
                :see-also [:feedback :telemetry]}
  :audit       {:theme :orient :purpose "Show audit-log entries."}
  :feedback {:theme :orient
             :purpose "Read feedback (usability sessions): a user works in the app while a second window records every click (point, element, dialog — a click on plain text or empty space is marked as one nothing answers), which fields she fills (length only), where she copies text from and pastes it into (place and length, never the text) and which pages she opens, and takes her notes per step (feeling leicht/geht/schwer + text) and a closing SUS questionnaire. Bare = the sessions (own; admin: all, --benutzer narrows); `feedback <id>` = the timeline with every note at its step and what was open when it was written (`offen:` the open dialog and the app's interface variables, e.g. rechnung=…), manual transfers copy → paste (⇄ — a field she fills by hand that the app could have filled), click series/rage clicks (↻), elements that answer clicks but carry no role (◌, an accessibility finding), the SUS score. Started by the user herself: top right, click her user name → Feedback. The admin marks a session's feedback as dealt with: `feedback <id> --erledigt` (`--offen` reopens it); bare, `--offen`/`--erledigt` list only those — the same as Admin Settings → Feedback in the GUI. `feedback <id> --szenario` turns the last error (or `--ereignis <id>`) and the last click before it into a replay scenario (sutra.types.feedback-szenario): the action, a smallest world it installs in, the call the window sent with stand-in values, and what was assumed; `--to <ABSOLUTE file>` writes it, tests/scenario/feedback_replay_test.clj replays every file in tests/scenario/feedback/ and dev.suite traces it."
             :when "Judging whether a real user is served by a flow — read what she actually did and said instead of guessing; the evidence behind a usability verdict."
             :examples ["feedback" "feedback --benutzer anna" "feedback <id>"
                        "feedback <id> --json" "feedback --offen"
                        "feedback <id> --erledigt"
                        "feedback <id> --szenario --to /abs/tests/scenario/feedback/<id>.edn"]
             :see-also [:login-audit]}
  :login-audit {:theme :orient
                :purpose "The LOGIN-EVENT trail: every authentication attempt against this instance — success or failure — newest first, with the attempted identity, provider (password/spnego), outcome and failure reason. Written on the shared `establish-session!` provider seam into an append-only `login_log`; admin-only. This is what answers 'who signed in, when' and 'did this Kerberos principal ever succeed' empirically (which `sessions` — only what is currently OPEN — cannot). NOT the LLM `audit` verb."
                :when "Investigating who has (or has not) logged in, chasing a failed-login/SPNEGO problem, or a security review of sign-ins. Bare = newest attempts; `login-audit <user>` or `--user` narrows to one identity; `--failed` (or `--outcome success|failure`) filters; `--provider password|spnego` the mechanism; `--limit` caps."
                :examples ["login-audit" "login-audit milo" "login-audit --failed"
                           "login-audit --provider spnego --limit 50"]
                :see-also [:audit :access :sso]}
  :replay-history {:theme :operate
                   :purpose "Replays the schema history's whole-world texts (one whole Ω_auth per schema commit, from before the ontology was kept per resource) into per-resource history: a version per resource where it changed, the change log, the states. Afterwards a resource's History reaches back as far as its branch's. Runs in the background: `--start` is a dry run (nothing written), `--apply` writes, `--verify` checks every old text against what its branch reads now, `--repair` resets every branch a replay alone wrote, replays it again and drops the versions nothing holds, `--drop-texts` deletes the texts of every branch whose resources now hold all of them (each verified first), `--dedupe` makes every resource's versions of one text one version (every world checked before and after), bare shows the last run's status per branch."
                   :when "Once after deploying per-resource storage: `--start` and read the report, then `--apply`. A second run finds nothing (:nothing). The old texts stay until removed on their own."
                   :examples ["replay-history --start" "replay-history" "replay-history --apply --yes"]
                   :see-also [:restore-point]}
  :restore-point {:theme :operate
                  :purpose "The DATA plane's restore points: `restore-point` lists them, `--label \"x\"` takes one, `--to <id>` rolls every versioned relation back to it. The store keeps row history already, so a point is a number and the rollback is a versioned write — no dump, no copy, and Postgres is never stopped."
                  :when "Before a risky build/install, take one; to undo a bad build's writes, roll back to it. NOT a full backup: the control plane (ontology, principals, sessions) carries no row versions and needs the deploy's own artifact."
                  :examples ["restore-point" "restore-point --label \"vor dem re-import\""
                             "restore-point --to 3 --yes"]
                  :see-also [:lineage-rollback :dataset :runs]}
  :profile     {:theme :operate
                :purpose "The SAMPLING profiler: every thread's stack on a fixed interval plus the CPU it burned, and markers carrying the facts a stack cannot (rows, bytes). Answers where a request's time ACTUALLY went, including the leaf nobody wrapped and the response pipeline the function tracer is barred from."
                :when "A request is slow and `trace` says SQL is only a fraction of it. `profile --on`, reproduce, then bare `profile` for the breakdown by domain and self frame, or `profile --save <file>` for a Gecko profile that opens in profiler.firefox.com with the call tree, flame graph and marker timeline."
                :patterns
                ["It SAMPLES, so unlike `trace --fns` it needs no prefixes, wraps nothing, and sees the expensive leaf nobody suspected."
                 "--on also arms the SQL tracer: the statement markers are read from its event stream instead of proxying JDBC twice."
                 "Markers are the other half: Request (bytes on the wire), SQL (statement + shape), Pool (the wait for a connection), Rows (per bound object type), Encode and Gzip."]
                :examples ["profile --on" "profile" "profile --limit 25"
                           "profile --save /tmp/door.json --label \"app door\"" "profile --off"]
                :see-also [:trace :health :runs]}
  :trace       {:theme :operate
                :purpose "Arm/stream the live SQL execution tracer (backend + egress agent) — the per-statement trace behind a stuck or slow install/action/build."
                :when "Diagnosing why a build/install/action hangs or misfires: `trace --on`, reproduce, `trace --follow` to watch statements land; `trace --kill <seq>` (or `--kill-all`) stops one in-flight statement the cooperative build-cancel can't; `trace --off` disarms."
                :patterns
                ["A statement slower than 500 ms while armed (`--on --slow-ms <n>` sets the bar) keeps its WHOLE text and its parameters; its event line names `--explain <seq>`."
                 "`trace --explain <seq>` runs that statement once more as EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) in a READ ONLY transaction that is rolled back, and answers the plan nodes with the most SELF time — estimated vs actual rows, loops, filter. `trace --kept` lists what can be explained."]
                :examples ["trace --on" "trace --kept" "trace --explain 412" "trace --explain 412 --limit 25"]
                :see-also [:watch :health :runs :profile]}
  :access      {:theme :orient
                :purpose "Admin-only consolidated access-control snapshot: every principal with its role, auth source (password/spnego/ldap) and marking clearances; the role→operation RBAC legend; each project's grants + owned-resource count; the marking registry with who is cleared; and the effective principal→project→operations matrix. Read-only, no secrets. `access <user>` drills into one principal; `access --project <p>` into one project's grants."
                :examples ["access" "access alma" "access --project werft" "access --json"]
                :see-also [:projects :grant :audit]}
  :llm         {:theme :orient
                :purpose "LLM cost + model-rate group. `llm models` lists the register (+ --history for the dated rate timeline); `llm register <id> --in <$/1M> --out <$/1M>` sets a model's current price; `llm reprice <id> --in --out --effective <date>` records a DATED rate (backdate it to reprice history — the recompute); `llm unregister <id>` drops a model; `llm cost [--since <d>] [--until <d>] [--today] [--caller <substr>] [--per <type>] [--calls]` is the money view (tokens × the rate in force at each call, from the llm_model_rates ledger), broken down by resource and by model over a day window."
                :when "Onboarding a model a transform needs, changing a price, repricing past calls after a wrong rate, or answering \"what did this LLM run cost today?\" / \"what did the email pipeline cost?\"."
                :patterns
                ["Cost is a VIEW over the token amounts logged on llm_call_log × the dated llm_model_rates ledger — nothing is frozen, so correcting a rate reprices history automatically."
                 "Rates are a direct token price: currency per 1,000,000 tokens (providers quote this way); --currency defaults to USD."
                 "`llm cost` scopes to a period with `--since`/`--until` (inclusive YYYY-MM-DD) or `--today`, and to one pipeline with `--caller <substr>` (matches a resource RID or its resolved name, e.g. `--caller parse`)."
                 "`llm cost` names the exact `llm reprice … --effective <ts>` command for any call it finds unpriced."
                 "`llm cost --run <run-id>` is the INVOICE for ONE build — the counterpart of the `build estimate` that preceded it (prefix-matched, so the 8-char id from `build list` is enough). It can only see calls logged WITH a run id; the python runtime started stamping one on 2026-08-06, so older calls answer as no match."]
                :examples ["llm models" "llm models --history"
                           "llm register grok-4.3 --in 1.25 --out 2.50"
                           "llm reprice grok-4.3 --in 1.25 --out 2.50 --effective 2026-07-04"
                           "llm cost --today" "llm cost --today --caller parse"
                           "llm cost --since 2026-07-01 --until 2026-07-23 --per email --calls"]
                :see-also [:describe]}
  :blob        {:theme :inspect
                :purpose "Download a content-addressed blob (attachment / PDF / media) by sha256 to a file."
                :examples ["blob <sha256> --out invoice.pdf"]}
  :egress-policy {:theme :inspect
                  :purpose "The network egress policies: each address and port with its state (pending, active, paused, revoked), who proposed and decided it, which sources use it, and what sources declare with no policy over it. Paused means approved but blocked on the host."
                  :when "When a source cannot reach its system, or before opening a route."
                  :examples ["source egress policy"]
                  :see-also [:egress :egress-policy-propose :egress-policy-approve]}
  :egress-policy-propose {:theme :write
                          :purpose "Propose a network egress policy. It stays pending, and a source using it fails, until an administrator approves it."
                          :examples ["source egress policy propose 192.0.2.226 8080 --why 'Telefon-Broker'"]
                          :see-also [:egress-policy :egress-policy-approve]}
  :agent-proxy-allowlist {:theme :write
                          :purpose "Set the CIDR blocks and ports an agent proxy may reach (admin), whatever its sources name. It reaches a linked agent at once, any other when it links. A local file on the agent host can narrow it further."
                          :examples ["source egress agent-allowlist nas 192.0.2.71/32:445"]
                          :see-also [:egress-policy-propose :egress]}
  :egress-policy-approve {:theme :write
                          :purpose "Approve a network egress policy (admin). It reaches the firewalls on the next refresh, unless the host has paused that address: then it shows as paused."
                          :examples ["source egress policy approve 192.0.2.226:8080"]
                          :see-also [:egress-policy :egress-policy-revoke]}
  :egress-policy-revoke {:theme :write
                         :purpose "Revoke a network egress policy (admin). Sources using it fail at once, and the kernel rule closes on the next refresh."
                         :examples ["source egress policy revoke 192.0.2.226:8080"]
                         :see-also [:egress-policy :egress-policy-approve]}
  :egress-policy-adopt {:theme :write
                        :purpose "Turn every address the sources declare into an active policy (admin), except those named, which stay pending. From the first policy on, anything uncovered is blocked."
                        :when "Once, to start enforcing without taking anything that runs today away."
                        :examples ["source egress policy adopt --except 192.0.2.226:8080"]
                        :see-also [:egress-policy]}
  :egress      {:theme :inspect
                :purpose "Per egress agent: reachability, key fingerprint, uptime, open write transactions, trace state, and the kernel allowlist derived from ITS sources' :egress — the host:ports its firewall pins; each endpoint's scope (agent vs agent+worker) is shown. Read-only."
                :examples ["egress"]
                :see-also [:sources :datasources]}
  :sources     {:theme :inspect
                :purpose "List the external sources declared on the branch: type, read-only?/egress, and whether a credential is sealed."
                :when "First step of exploring an external DB — see what's connected before drilling in with `source`/`source-tables`."
                :examples ["sources" "sources --json"]
                :see-also [:source :source-tables :egress]}
  :source      {:theme :inspect
                :purpose "One source's (redacted) connection spec + a LIVE reachability probe."
                :when
                "To confirm a source is reachable and read-only before you explore or import it."
                :examples ["source mro"]
                :see-also [:sources :source-tables :peek]}
  :source-tables {:theme :inspect
                  :purpose "The source's table + view names, optional --like substring filter. Routed through the egress agent for egress sources — the core holds no credential."
                  :when "Discover WHAT a freshly-connected external DB contains, before writing any :external-source :query."
                  :examples ["source-tables mro" "source-tables mro --like wo"]
                  :see-also [:source-columns :peek :source-graph]}
  :source-columns {:theme :inspect
                   :purpose "One table's columns — raw vendor SQL type + the folded Sutra-OS ontology-type an imported field defaults to."
                   :when "Before authoring an :external-source mapping: learn the real column names/types so the :query and field types are right first time."
                   :examples ["source-columns mro WO_STAMM"]
                   :see-also [:source-tables :peek]}
  :peek        {:theme :inspect
                :purpose "Up to --limit LIVE sample rows of one external table. Read-only + capped in the SQL server-side — never a full-table fetch; distinct from a dataset `query` (which reads already-imported rows)."
                :when "To eyeball real values/shape of an external table before committing to import it."
                :examples ["peek mro WO_STAMM" "peek mro AC_STAMM --limit 5"]
                :see-also [:source-columns :source-sql]}
  :source-sql  {:theme :inspect
                :purpose "Run ONE ad-hoc read-only SELECT/WITH against the LIVE external source, capped — the 'build with the schema you just learned' step. The backend read-only wall rejects anything but a single SELECT/WITH (admin-only)."
                :when "To validate a JOIN/WHERE/shape against the real source BEFORE baking it into an :external-source :query."
                :patterns
                ["Prototype here, then paste the proven SELECT into the object-type's :external-source :query in the .ent."]
                :anti-patterns
                [{:rule "Never a write — only SELECT/WITH is accepted; INSERT/UPDATE/DDL are rejected by the read-only guard."}]
                :examples ["source-sql mro \"SELECT TOP 5 WO_NR, WO_TEXT FROM WO_STAMM\""]
                :see-also [:peek :source-graph]}
  :writeback-check {:theme :inspect
                    :purpose "Hold ONE writeback target table against its :writeback allowlist BEFORE a new write action relies on it: allowlisted columns exist, :pk reaches an attribute, :pk is the table's primary key, triggers carry SET NOCOUNT ON, datetime columns in the conflict check, ISNULL/COALESCE over writable columns in the sync SQL, :log :none, OPEN_USER/CURRENTUSER left empty. Read-only; exit 1 on a hard finding."
                    :when "Before adding or widening a write action on an external table."
                    :examples ["writeback check mro ANGEBOT"]
                    :see-also [:source-columns :source-sql]}
  :source-graph {:theme :inspect
                 :purpose "The source's foreign-key graph: from-table.col → to-table.col edges, optionally anchored at --table."
                 :when "To learn how the external tables relate before modeling links — which columns are the join keys."
                 :examples ["source-graph mro" "source-graph mro --table WO_STAMM"]
                 :see-also [:source-tables :source-columns]}
  :test-transform {:theme :inspect
                   :purpose "Runs a transform's UNIT TESTS: each `:tests` entry is `{:name … :inputs {alias [rows]} :expect [rows] :output?}` — inputs entered by hand, the transform's own query or code, the output expected of them. Nothing is read from the store and nothing is written: a :sql query runs over the test inputs landed as scratch tables (the input's declared columns), a code transform gets them as its inputs. The output is compared with :expect as a multiset on the columns :expect names (10, 10.0 and 10M are one value; a blank is null); an empty :expect demands no rows."
                   :when "Whenever you change a transform's logic — and to pin down cases that must keep holding (known pairs, edge cases a fix was made for). A proposal that touches a transform with :tests does not land while one fails: merge-proposal runs them on the source branch."
                   :patterns ["`sutra --branch <b> transform test <name>` runs the branch's version — the one the proposal would land."
                              "A test names only the columns it is about: a column it leaves out may be anything."]
                   :see-also [:preview-transform :merge-proposal]}
  :preview-transform {:theme :inspect
                      :purpose "WITHOUT committing a transaction. Works for EVERY language — a :python or :clojure preview really runs the transform in its executor (incl. its LLM/egress). `--limit` shapes the OUTPUT only; the transform reads its inputs WHOLE unless you pass `--sample N`, or it reads a language model — then each input is sampled to --limit rows, so an LLM preview costs a few calls, not the corpus. Every sampled input is named in the result (`input-sample`, `input-caps`). The direct 'why 0 rows?' AND 'will this transform blow up at run time?' probe."
                      :when "Before you commit + deploy + full-backfill a transform change: run it here on a sample FIRST. A :sql JOIN that silently drops every row shows count 0; a :python or :clojure transform that hits a bad config key, an uncastable decimal, or an unreachable source shows that error verbatim (a clojure failure points at its own source line) — cheaply, instead of failing on prod mid-build."
                      :patterns
                      ["Iterate the transform's :query/code here until `count` is right and there's no :error, THEN push the fixed .ent and run-import — never debug a run-time failure by deploy+backfill."]
                      :anti-patterns
                      [{:rule "Don't diff a preview taken before a change against one taken after — it is an UNORDERED sample."
                        :why "The result has no ORDER BY and caps at 1000 rows (default 50), so which rows come back depends on the plan and nothing stable: on prod the SAME unchanged query run twice overlapped 0 of 50 rows on a 5,130-row result. A changed join predicate can reorder the sample while the full result is identical — which reads exactly like a regression. To prove a query edit is semantics-preserving, compare WHOLE result sets; if you must use preview, run a control (the same query twice) first to establish the noise floor."}
                       {:rule
                        "Don't read a SAMPLED preview over a JOIN as the transform's answer."
                        :why "A sampled input (`--sample N`, or an LLM transform's default sample of --limit rows) hands the transform N rows per input (`input-caps: [{input …, kept 3, total 18746}]`), so anything that joins finds nothing and returns nulls — which looks like a logic bug. Drop `--sample` (non-LLM) or raise it to the input's total."}]
                      :examples ["preview-transform reconcile-buchung-rechnung"
                                 "preview-transform observe-prices --limit 20"
                                 "preview-transform parse_invoices --limit 5"]
                      :see-also [:jobs :query :run-import :lint]}
  :media-set-items {:theme :inspect
                    :purpose "List what one media set holds: each item's name, address and format, the first 200 of them, with the total."
                    :when "After a sync of a media set, to see what it took. A virtual item is listed like any other; its bytes stay in the source."
                    :examples ["media-set items plaene"]}
  :transform-check {:theme :inspect
                    :purpose "Run the Data-Health checks bound to ONE transform NODE. The check runner keys checks by lineage node and resolves a transform to its output `d_<name>` table, so these checks grade the transform's OUTPUT. Filters the check list to node-kind transform + node-key <name> and runs each; a transform with no checks is a note, not an error."
                    :when "After a transform change, to confirm its output still holds (row-count/freshness/null-%/custom). Author the checks with a `:health-checks` rider `:on :transform` (see `describe :health-checks`); `health-check list` shows all checks across every node."
                    :examples ["transform check enrich-orders"]
                    :see-also [:health-check-list :health-check-run :preview-transform :health]}
  :eval        {:theme :inspect
                :purpose
                "Run an eval suite as Evals does: one target (:function, :agent, or :action planned dry — a transform is measured through a Function that shares its core module), test cases (:cases, or :cases-from an object set: {:object-type :where :input {col property} :expected {col property}}, a property may be {:link side :property p} or {:val x}), evaluators judged PER CASE (an evaluator with :intermediate \"<name>\" judges what a Function exposed with intermediate(name, value) — intermediate parameters; `eval cases` shows them per case, a results dataset gets them in an optional intermediates column). A case passes when every metric passes in every iteration; the run reports the case pass rate, each metric's value, and keeps every case (`eval cases <run>`). Also: list suites; history — every run and each metric's course over them (`--by model|version` averages per model or prompt version, the metrics dashboard); compare two stored runs side by side (metrics a → b, then every case whose verdict or output changed)."
                :when "To measure whether a technical decision is right against human-confirmed truth. Declare a suite that should be reviewed with the schema under `:eval-suites` in <project>.evals.ent (projection push); `eval save <file.edn>` keeps an ad-hoc one on the branch. A suite is checked before it runs: an unknown column, property, link or evaluator is refused with a did-you-mean."
                :anti-patterns
                [{:rule "Don't gate a suite on a metric's mean."
                  :why "The objective is per test case: {:expect true}, {:min n}, {:max n}. A boolean metric passes on true by default; a numeric one (levenshtein, rouge, llm-judge) needs an objective to pass or fail. There is no threshold on the whole metric — the run's case pass rate and `eval compare` (PASS → FAIL per case) say whether it got worse."}
                 {:rule "Don't compare amounts and dates with :exact-match when the source rounds."
                  :why "Use :numeric-range (:tolerance / :tolerance-pct) and :temporal-range (:tolerance-days); text that may differ by a typo is :levenshtein with :objective {:max 1}."}
                 {:rule "Don't run an LLM-backed target once."
                  :why "An agent, or a function with a :language-model input, defaults to 3 iterations; σ shows the spread. --iterations overrides."}
                 {:rule "Don't compare models with a run per model by hand."
                  :why "An experiment is one named grid: --grid param=a|b (repeatable), one run per combination, each value overriding that input in every case. Parameters are the target's inputs, `model` (every language-model input and use-llm literal) and `version`; a language-model input by its name swaps only that input. --preview lists the combinations and costs nothing; `eval history <suite> --experiment <name> --by <param>` averages per value."}
                 {:rule "Don't answer cases one at a time."
                  :why "A run answers 10 cases at once, as Evals does; --parallel n (or :parallel in the suite) lowers it against a provider's rate limit or raises it."}]
                :examples ["eval suites" "eval my-suite" "eval my-suite --parallel 4"
                           "eval my-suite --meta prompt=v2 --meta ticket=123"
                           "eval agent-tests --grid model=claude-opus-5-5|claude-sonnet-5-5"
                           "eval my-suite --experiment prompts --grid model=a|b --grid kontext=x|y --preview"
                           "eval history my-suite --experiment prompts --by model"
                           "eval cases <run-id> --failing"
                           "eval history my-suite --by version"
                           "eval compare <run-a> <run-b>"]
                :see-also [:new-function :action :run-function]}
  :streams     {:theme :inspect
                :purpose "The declared STREAMS — datasets that carry a hot buffer in front of their archived rows. Per stream: partitions, whether it is a CDC changelog or a plain log, how many records are still buffered, how many rows are archived, and how many live subscribers are attached."
                :when "Orienting on the real-time side of the instance, or asking whether archiving is keeping up (a growing `buffered` with a still archived count is the tell)."
                :examples ["stream list"]
                :see-also [:stream-show :dataset-preview :transactions]}
  :stream-show {:theme :inspect
                :purpose "One stream in full: its partition keys and CDC primary key, per-partition END OFFSETS (the offset the next record will take) and buffer depth, and where each consumer — `archive` (into cold storage) and `index` (into the Ontology) — currently stands."
                :when "Diagnosing latency or a backlog. A consumer cursor far behind the end offsets is the measurement; `stream flush` is the manual catch-up."
                :examples ["stream show sensor_readings"]
                :see-also [:streams :stream-offsets :stream-flush]}
  :stream-offsets {:theme :inspect
                   :purpose "The offset each partition will write NEXT. Offsets only ever move forward — retention trimming does not walk them back — so a consumer can hold one across a restart."
                   :examples ["stream offsets sensor_readings"]
                   :see-also [:stream-tail :stream-show]}
  :stream-publish {:theme :author-data
                   :purpose "Push records into a stream: `stream publish <name> --record '{\"c\":1}'` or `--file rows.json` (one object or an array). Records are validated against the stream's declared columns and the batch is admitted or refused WHOLE — a partial publish would leave a changelog nobody could reason about."
                   :when "Push-based ingestion — an event-shaped feed that has no table to sync from. A record's key columns decide its partition, which is what keeps updates to one key in order."
                   :anti-patterns
                   [{:rule "Don't compute in the publisher what the pipeline should compute."
                     :why "A stream ingests as-is, like any other Source Manager. Trimming, casting and key-building belong in a transform, which is in git and reviewable; a publisher that reshapes is a rule nobody can diff."}]
                   :examples
                   ["stream publish sensor_readings --record '{\"sensor_id\":\"s1\",\"temperature\":21.5}'"
                    "stream publish sensor_readings --file readings.json"]
                   :see-also [:stream-tail :stream-view :stream-flush]}
  :stream-listen-udp {:theme :author-data
                      :purpose "Forward UDP datagrams into a stream: `stream listen-udp <name> --port 5514 [--bind IP] [--batch N] [--flush-ms N]`. Each datagram becomes one record, unparsed: `message`, `encoding` (`utf-8`, or `base64` for binary), `source_ip`, `source_port`, `received_at`; the stream must declare these columns. Runs until stopped and is the one stream verb that stays up, so run it as a service on a host the senders can reach. While the platform is away it holds records in memory, up to 100000, then drops and counts them. A refusal, also an expired login (401), ends it with exit 1: restart it through the service manager."
                      :when "Devices that only send UDP (a switch's syslog, SNMP traps, sFlow) into a stream, from a network the platform cannot reach. The agent proxy does not carry this: it dials TCP outwards from the platform and cannot receive."
                      :examples ["stream listen-udp switch_events --port 5514"
                                 "stream listen-udp switch_events --port 5514 --bind 10.0.0.5 --batch 200 --flush-ms 500"]
                      :see-also [:stream-publish :stream-tail :stream-show]}
  :stream-tail {:theme :inspect
                :purpose "Read the raw changelog BY OFFSET — one partition at a time, oldest first. This is the replayable read: the same offsets always answer with the same records, until retention drops them."
                :when "Asking what actually arrived, in what order. `stream view` answers what the records MEAN once resolved; this answers what was published."
                :examples ["stream tail sensor_readings"
                           "stream tail sensor_readings --partition 1 --from 40 --limit 20"]
                :see-also [:stream-view :stream-offsets]}
  :stream-view {:theme :inspect
                :purpose "The CDC CURRENT VIEW: archived rows with the still-buffered records folded on top, resolved to one row per primary key with retractions applied. The hybrid read — cold storage alone is minutes stale, the buffer alone has only the tail."
                :when "Asking what a stream currently SAYS, as opposed to what it has published. For a stream with no primary key there is nothing to resolve and this is just the records."
                :examples ["stream view sensor_readings --limit 50"]
                :see-also [:stream-tail :dataset-preview]}
  :stream-flush {:theme :operate
                 :purpose "Catch one stream up NOW: index its buffered records into the Ontology, then archive them into the dataset. The background loop does exactly this on the stream's own cadence; this is the manual door onto it."
                 :when "Developing against a stream, or after a backlog. The order is not optional — archiving is what trims the buffer, so indexing has to happen first or records are retired before the Ontology sees them."
                 :examples ["stream flush sensor_readings"]
                 :see-also [:stream-show :run-import]}
  :stream-reset {:theme :operate
                 :purpose "Clear an ingest stream: its buffer, its archive dataset (a new DELETE transaction) and every consumer cursor. A stream a transform writes is refused. The end offsets keep climbing — a consumer holding an offset will not silently be handed different data under it. Downstream pipelines must be replayed."
                 :when "Stream development, or a poisoned buffer. Not for production: the existing records are gone."
                 :examples ["stream reset sensor_readings --yes"]
                 :see-also [:stream-show :stream-flush]}
  :transactions {:theme :inspect
                 :purpose "The Catalog's typed transaction log for a dataset (SNAPSHOT/APPEND/DELETE per build), newest first."
                 :examples ["transactions raw_messages" "transactions email --limit 20"]
                 :anti-patterns
                 [{:rule "Don't date a TRANSFORM by the transactions of its output."
                   :why "A link dataset or a supplement on an object type has no transaction of its own, so the answer is empty — and for the rest it dates the write, not the run. The run is the transform's job in the ledger: `lineage facts <t> --detail last-run,last-success,last-failure` reads it for every transform alike, in one call."}]
                 :see-also [:consumption :delta :lineage-facts]}
  :dataset-preview {:theme :inspect
                    :purpose "Reveals columns a transform EMITS but the object-type shape doesn't declare (they land in the table yet never surface via `query`, which projects only declared attributes)."
                    :examples ["dataset preview parse-invoices --limit 5"
                               "dataset-preview rechnung"]
                    :see-also [:transactions :query :describe]}
  :consumption {:theme :inspect
                :purpose "A consumer transform's input consumer-pointers — which input datasets it consumed and at which snapshot."
                :examples ["consumption classify_events"]
                :anti-patterns
                [{:rule "Don't read `consumption` as WHO reads a transform's output."
                  :why "It answers the other direction: what one transform read, and at which snapshot. Who reads an output — transforms, backed types, links, apps, actions, functions, eval suites, automations, exports — is `lineage facts <node> --down 1`."}
                 {:rule "Don't date a TRANSFORM by the transactions of its output."
                  :why "A link dataset or a supplement on an object type has no transaction of its own, so the answer is empty — and for the rest it dates the write, not the run. The run is the transform's job in the ledger: `lineage facts <t> --detail last-run,last-success,last-failure` reads it for every transform alike, in one call."}]
                :see-also [:transactions :delta :lineage-facts]}
  :delta       {:theme :inspect
                :purpose "How many input rows were added/modified since a transform last consumed it, plus the {low,high} boundary."
                :examples ["delta classify_events raw_messages"]
                :see-also [:transactions :consumption]}

  :install     {:theme :author-schema
                :purpose "Stage a declarative schema (an inline :schema map) as a create/update/delete reconcile against the live ontology. Does NOT commit. RETIRED as a FILE door: `sutra foo.ent` (and a runbook carrying an `:install` step) is refused — an .ent reaches the ontology by being COMMITTED and PUSHED, where the same install runs as `ci/sutra-publish` after scope/retire/lint/plan. The step itself stays live for the in-session authoring verbs (`ontology object-type create`, `transform create`, `source-add`, `transform edit`)."
                :when "Reached through the create/edit verbs, not by hand. To ship an authored .ent: `projection pull ontologie` (types) or `projection pull code-repository` (code) on a branch, edit, `projection push --preview`, `projection push`, `commit` — never `git push`."
                :patterns
                ["Author the .ent in its pulled checkout, preview with `projection push <text> --preview` (non-mutating), then push — don't guess fields from memory."]
                :anti-patterns
                [{:rule "Don't hand-craft create/update/delete steps when a declarative .ent reconcile expresses the target state."
                  :why "The reconcile diffs desired-vs-live for you; imperative steps drift from the real schema."}
                 {:rule "Don't assume re-installing an object-type with an attribute REMOVED will drop that column — a drop-only edit is often silently skipped, so it does NOT edit cleanly through install."
                  :why "The object-type reconcile is a COVERAGE check: it stages :update-object-type when a declared attribute is added or its type/opts change, but it TOLERATES attributes that are live yet absent from your declaration (so interface-contributed columns don't churn). A declaration that ONLY drops an attribute — everything else matching live exactly — is treated as already-satisfied and skipped, so the column lingers; a `:title`-only change isn't reconciled at all. This bites `:external-source` object-types hardest: their columns are query-inferred, so editing the :query to stop emitting a column does NOT drop it either. A drop lands only when some OTHER change forces the :update-object-type replace — always confirm with `describe <type>` after `commit`."}]
                :see-also [:diff-schema :commit :review :object-type :describe]}
  :commit      {:theme :author-schema
                :purpose "Commit the staged batch: merge your staging branch onto its parent in one structured commit. Conflicts leave both branches untouched."
                :when "After `review`/`check` pass. In the REPL prefer `save` (the gated commit)."
                :patterns
                ["Treat commit like merging to a protected master — run `review`/`check` first so what lands is reviewed, not guessed."
                 "THREE DOORS LAND WORK, and they are not alternatives. `commit` is the raw one and lands the STAGED BATCH the in-session authoring verbs built. `save` is the same act, gated (check + typed confirm) — prefer it interactively. Neither is the door for FILES: an .ent/.enta you edited in a pulled checkout lands through `projection push`, and on a protected branch these two are refused outright."]
                :anti-patterns
                [{:rule "Don't assume the commit gate only validates YOUR staged delta — the relational checks (e.g. link-types-valid?) re-validate the WHOLE resulting schema, so a PRE-EXISTING violation in a link you never touched fail-closes your unrelated commit."
                  :why "The FK-backing invariant was enforced after some links already violated it; until every violator is migrated to {:fk}/{:fk-resolve}, NO schema commit succeeds. Run `ontology lint link-fk-backing` FIRST to see the full blocking set — a rejection naming links you didn't stage is this, not a bug in your change. Clear them all in ONE commit."}
                 {:rule "Don't expect a commit to populate anything — it lands schema DDL and launches NO build."
                  :why "The whole commit path (commit-staged! → install-schema-delta! + fk-link reregister + bumped-transform watermark reset) contains no importer call, and neither the REST handler nor the FE do. A new object-type's table lands EMPTY; filling it needs a separate `run-import`. The 'build that always follows a commit' comes from the build_schedules scheduler firing on its own cadence, and it LOOKS causal only because `runs.parent_run_id` is the latest-succeeded run of ANY kind — so whichever scheduled build fires next inherits your commit's run as its parent. That is correlation; don't read a commit→build trigger out of `build show <id>`."}]
                :see-also [:review :check :ontology-lint :run-import]}
  :review      {:theme :author-schema
                :purpose "Show the staged diff grouped by entity class (with real names) and gate y/N before commit."
                :see-also [:check :commit]}
  :confirm     {:theme :author-schema :purpose "Interactive y/N gate for a destructive step."}
  :datasources {:theme :author-schema
                :purpose "Register datasource connection specs the sources/transforms read from."
                :patterns
                ["Pin/validate the incoming column types explicitly so a change in the upstream source schema fails the build loudly instead of silently mis-mapping a column."]
                :see-also [:source]}
  :source-add  {:theme :author-schema
                :purpose "Register a NEW external JDBC source: seal its credential to the egress agent and stage+commit the :sources entry — so exploration needs no .ent. --egress is derived from --url; --user/--password prompt masked when omitted (plaintext never leaves the CLI). JDBC types only (postgres/mysql/mssql)."
                :when "The zero-.ent way to connect a source you then explore (source-tables/peek) and import (sync). For rest/imap or richer config, declare the :sources block in `<project>.ingest.ent` and `projection push` it — a text carries the CONNECTOR — then set its credential with `source secret <name>`."
                :patterns
                ["The credential is sealed RSA-OAEP to the agent and stored as :secret-cipher in the store — only the egress agent can decrypt it; the core never holds plaintext."
                 "After adding, the host egress allowlist re-derives from live sources on its next refresh tick (or immediately in dev); `egress` shows the current allowlist."]
                :examples
                ["source-add mro --type mssql --url \"jdbc:sqlserver://db.example.internal:1433;database=SalesDB;encrypt=false\" --user sa"
                 "source-add warehouse --type postgres --url jdbc:postgresql://10.0.0.5:5432/wh --user ro --stage"]
                :see-also [:sources :source :source-tables :sync :egress :source-secret]}
  :source-adopt {:theme :author-schema
                 :purpose "Take a table of a source in as a virtual dataset — and, with --as <Type>, an object type backed by it — in one change: <source> <table> [--key a,b] [--into <dataset>|--dataset <name>] [--project p] [--preview] [--land|--stage]."
                 :when "A connector table should be readable (and queryable as objects) in place, without a sync or a transform in between."}
  :image-push {:theme :operate
               :purpose "Push an image for bring-your-own-container: <name>:<tag> <archive.tar> --project P. The archive is `docker save`/`podman save` output (one image); its config and layers go up as blobs (≤ 128 MB each), then the server checks the image requirements — numeric non-root USER, linux/amd64, a tag other than `latest`."
               :when "A transform needs a program Python cannot bring (a solver binary, COBOL, an old runtime): build the image locally with `--platform linux/amd64`, push it, then name it in the decorator with `.with_container(container_image=…, container_tag=…)`. Container workflows are off until `settings container-workflows true`."}
  :images {:theme :operate
           :purpose "List the pushed images: name, tag, project, user, layers and size."
           :when "Before naming an image in with_container, or to see what a project holds."}
  :image-delete {:theme :operate
                 :purpose "Delete a pushed image: <name>:<tag>. Its layers go with the next blob sweep once nothing else holds them."
                 :when "An image version no transform names any more."}
  :packages {:theme :project
             :purpose "List the ready-made packages: the version offered, the version installed, and whether an upgrade waits."
             :when "Before `package install` or `package upgrade`, to see what there is and what is behind."}
  :package-install {:theme :project
                    :purpose "Install a package: <id> [--preview] [--land|--stage] — on a klein trunk it lands at once."
                    :when "Starting a concern from a ready-made package instead of writing its texts by hand."}
  :package-upgrade {:theme :project
                    :purpose "Move an installed package to the offered release, merged with what you changed since: <id> [--preview] [--mine|--theirs] [--land|--stage]."
                    :when "When `packages` says an upgrade waits; --preview first, a conflict is named element by element."}
  :product-publish {:theme :project
                    :purpose "Publish projects as a product release: <product> <version> --projects a,b — drafted, or released on --channel with --release; a version never changes."
                    :when "Handing a finished concern to another space, branch or instance as a versioned product."}
  :product-install {:theme :project
                    :purpose "Install a released product: <product> [version] with --suffix/--prefix to install it again, --bind input=local for its inputs, --lock, --channel, --auto-upgrade and a maintenance window."
                    :when "Taking a product in; --preview first, --with-linked installs the products it requires along."}
  :product-upgrade {:theme :project
                    :purpose "Move an installation to the newest release its channel offers (or a named version, also back), merged per element with your edits: <installation-id> [version] [--mine|--theirs]."
                    :when "When `product installations` names an upgrade; a locked installation changes only this way."}
  :product-uninstall {:theme :project
                      :purpose "Take an installation out: a strict staging removes what it installed and, once it lands, its documents; its projects stay, emptied."
                      :when "Retiring a product; --preview first shows what goes."}
  :product-configure {:theme :project
                      :purpose "Set how an installation is kept: --channel, --auto-upgrade or --manual, a maintenance window or --no-window."
                      :when "Letting an installation follow its channel by itself, inside the hours it may change."}
  :product-export {:theme :project
                   :purpose "Write a release as a bundle file, hashed and signed by this instance: <product> <version> [--out file]."
                   :when "Carrying a product to another instance; give it `product issuer` too so it can trust the signature."}
  :product-import {:theme :project
                   :purpose "Take in a bundle file another instance exported: its digest, the signature of a trusted issuer and the absence of credentials are checked."
                   :when "After `product trust <issuer> <key>` for the instance that exported it."}
  :source-secret {:theme :author-schema
                  :purpose "Seal a credential onto an EXISTING source: encrypt it to the egress agent (RSA-OAEP) and stage+commit ONLY the :secret-cipher — every other field of the source is left exactly as it is. --user/--password for a database source, --field/--value for a rest/imap one; an omitted value is prompted for, masked, so it never reaches shell history either."
                  :when "The other half of publishing sources through git. `<project>.ingest.ent` declares the CONNECTOR (type, endpoint, :egress, :access, cleartext :config) and `projection push` installs it — a push carrying `:secrets` or `:secret-cipher` is REFUSED by the scope check. This is how the secret gets in, once, out of band. Also the way to ROTATE one without a commit."
                  :patterns
                  ["Push the source, then seal it: `projection push` (declares it) → `source secret <name> --user U` (arms it) → `source show <name>` reports `sealed? true`."
                   "The staged update carries the cipher alone; `kernel/merge-existing` preserves every omitted field, so a rotation cannot revert a reviewed connector change."]
                  :anti-patterns [{:rule "Never put :secrets or :secret-cipher in a pushed .ent."
                                   :why "Plaintext in a commit is permanent and cloned by everyone with read access; a sealed cipher does not round-trip (the exporter omits it), so the next projection deletes the line and the tree flip-flops."}]
                  :examples ["source secret mro --user sa"
                             "source secret xai --field api-key"]
                  :see-also [:source-add :sources :source :egress]}
  :sync        {:theme :author-schema
                :purpose "Codegen + STAGE an :external-source object-type from an explored source table (or ad-hoc --sql). Introspects columns → one attribute each (folded type), generates the SELECT, binds :datasource + :primary-key. Leaves it staged for review/commit, then `run-import --only <type>`."
                :when "After exploring a source (source-tables/source-columns/peek): the one-command way to turn a table into an imported object-type without hand-writing the .ent :external-source block."
                :patterns
                ["The generated attributes auto-pull by name (derive-col) — no :column-map needed. Refine types/title/PK afterward if the defaults aren't right."]
                :examples ["sync mro WO_STAMM --as arbeitsauftrag --key wo_nr"
                           "sync mro --as auftrag_kurz --sql \"SELECT wo_nr, bezeichnung FROM WO_STAMM\" --key wo_nr"]
                :see-also [:source-columns :peek :source-sql :run-import]}

  :upload      {:theme :author-data
                :purpose "Land a local file: its bytes go to the blob CAS, ONE media-set row goes into a dataset (the media-column = its sha256, plus --set fields), and an optional ON-UPLOAD Action acknowledges it. Landing is not building."
                :when "You have a local file (CSV/PDF/image) to ingest through a :media-set transform. `bank-import` is the account-CSV preset over this."
                :patterns
                ["An upload ENDS AT THE FILE; the pipeline catches up on its own trigger. Do not reach for --build to make it feel finished."
                 "--on-upload <action> fires an Action per uploaded file, which is how the user gets an answer without waiting for a pipeline ('your file is here, processing follows'). Bind the file into its parameters with --arg; a value of {{media-reference}} becomes the sha256, {{filename}} the file's name, {{dataset}} where it landed."
                 "--set fills the dataset ROW, --arg fills the ACTION's parameters. Two schemas, two owners, deliberately not merged."
                 "If the Action fails the file IS STILL LANDED (blob + row are committed by then). Re-run the Action, do not re-upload — the CAS is content-addressed, so a second upload of the same bytes changes nothing."
                 "--build is the explicit 'and run it now' escape hatch, not what an upload means. Prefer a schedule with a :parent-updated trigger on the uploads dataset."]
                :examples ["upload data.csv --to raw_uploads --set jahr=2024,art=erfolgskonten"
                           "upload rechnung.pdf --to beleg_uploads --on-upload einreichung-anlegen --arg beleg={{media-reference}},dateiname={{filename}}"
                           "upload data.csv --to raw_uploads --build monatswert"]
                :see-also [:bank-import :dataset-write :action :schedule-create]}
  :bank-import {:theme :author-data
                  :purpose "Domain preset over `upload`: ingest an account-CSV from the accounting system — derives import_id from the file's content-hash + year, defaults the uploads dataset, and runs the scoped build that materialises :monatswert + :konto."
                  :when "Loading an account export from the accounting system into the accounting ontology. For any other file use the generic `upload`."
                  :examples ["bank-import konten_2024.csv"
                             "bank-import export.csv --jahr 2024 --art erfolgskonten"]
                  :see-also [:upload]}
  :principals  {:theme :author-data
                :purpose "Mint login principals (users) with auto or explicit passwords."
                :patterns
                ["For a login that owns long-lived schedules or pipelines, prefer a dedicated service user over a personal account so a change in one person's permissions can't break the pipeline."]
                :see-also [:grant]}
  :org-guest   {:theme :operate
                :purpose "Add or remove a guest of an organization: `org guest add|remove <organization> <principal> [--stage]`. A guest is inside the organization's walls without it being their primary organization (one each, a member in `:members`)."
                :when "Someone of another organization works in this one's spaces."
                :see-also [:org-show :org-list :grant]}
  :grant       {:theme :author-data
                :purpose "Grant or revoke a principal's role on a project (freigabe)."
                :patterns
                ["Manage access at the project level — a pipeline's or schedule's ability to run shouldn't hinge on one person's individual permissions."
                 "The grant list is the only evidence: after any grant or revoke, read `access --project <p>` and compare. It reports the CHECKED-OUT branch, which is also why a batch against a proposal branch must open with `checkout <name>`."]
                :anti-patterns
                [{:rule "Don't pass a username to `--to` — it takes a user-id UUID or a GROUP name, and resolves neither."
                  :why "`<x>` is taken verbatim as the `:principal`. `--to milo --revoke` against a grant stored as a UUID matches nothing yet prints `⊘ revoked milo …` and changes the list not at all — the success message is not evidence. `--to milo` without `--revoke` writes `{\"principal\":\"milo\"}`, read as a group name: an entry that grants nobody access and still looks plausible in the list. Pull the UUIDs from `access --json` (`principals[].user-id`) first."}
                 {:rule "Don't grant against `main` — the trunk is protected for grants too."
                  :why "It ends `✗ commit grant: branch 'main' is protected` (HTTP 403), the same guard as `git push` and the delete endpoint — a third channel people discover separately. Go through a branch: `branch <n>` → a second batch opening with `checkout <n>` → the grants → `propose --target main` → `approve` → `merge-proposal` (the source branch then turns Merged on its own)."}]
                :examples ["grant procurement --to @me --role ontology-editor"]
                :see-also [:principals :access :propose]}
  :sso         {:theme :operate
                :purpose "Configure SPNEGO/Kerberos SSO so Windows-domain users log in with their AD ticket (admin only)."
                :when "Set once per instance to enable domain login. `sso` / `sso status` shows the current (redacted) config; `sso config` sets realm/kdc/spn and the keytab. SSO users are then PRE-PROVISIONED with `register --external user@REALM`."
                :patterns
                ["Provide the keytab as a FILE path (--keytab); it is base64-encoded client-side and stored in-platform (kernel config), never on the command line."
                 "Realm/KDC/SPN are prompted interactively when omitted, so `sso config` alone walks you through it."]
                :examples ["sso status"
                           "sso config --realm EXAMPLE.LOCAL --kdc dc1.example.local --spn HTTP/sutra.example.local@EXAMPLE.LOCAL --keytab ./sutra.keytab --enable"]
                :see-also [:principals :grant :ldap]}
  :idp         {:theme :operate
                :purpose "Any identity provider the server has (LDAP, SPNEGO, …), driven by what it declares: list them, show one's status or its settings fields, set them as k=v, preview and sync its directory (admin only)."
                :when "When a provider is not `ldap` or `sso`, or to see which providers and settings this server has before configuring one. `idp` alone lists them; `idp <id> settings` names the fields `config` takes."
                :patterns
                ["`idp ldap config url=ldaps://dc1:636 base-dn=DC=example,DC=com --enable` — the field names are those `idp ldap settings` shows; a field the provider does not declare is refused by name."
                 "`idp ldap sync --dry-run` is the plan; `--disable-gone` disables users the directory no longer lists."]
                :examples ["idp" "idp spnego status" "idp ldap settings" "idp ldap sync --dry-run"]
                :see-also [:ldap :sso :principals]}
  :ldap        {:theme :operate
                :purpose "Sync AD/LDAP users + group memberships into Sutra-OS: federated principals + group-mapped project grants (admin only)."
                :when "The companion to `sso`: Kerberos proves WHO a user is, LDAP sync decides WHAT they may see. `ldap status` shows the redacted config; `ldap config` sets the DC connection + a group→role map; `ldap sync` reconciles (idempotent — creates only missing users, re-applies grants)."
                :patterns
                ["The group→role map keys AD group CNs to {:role … :projects […]} — a group not in the map is the allowlist boundary (its users are skipped)."
                 "`--group-map` takes an EDN FILE path or inline EDN; `ldap sync --dry-run` prints the plan (create/exists per user) without writing."
                 "The bind password is stored in-platform (kernel config), never echoed back; the directory is read READ-ONLY."
                 "The server plans and applies the sync (users, principals, grants, and — when the instance has a :group type with a :member-of link — the memberships of the directory's own groups; a membership in any other group is never touched); users the directory no longer lists are reported as :gone and only disabled with `--disable-gone` (needs a `disabled` attribute on :user)."]
                :anti-patterns
                [{:rule "Don't hand-register every domain user; put them in an AD group and let `ldap sync` reconcile."
                  :why "AD group membership IS the source of truth. Manual registration drifts."}]
                :examples ["ldap status"
                           "ldap config --url ldaps://dc1.example.com:636 --bind-dn 'CN=svc,CN=Users,DC=example,DC=com' --bind-pw secret --base-dn 'DC=example,DC=com' --realm EXAMPLE.COM --group-map ./groups.edn --enable"
                           "ldap sync --dry-run"]
                :see-also [:sso :principals :grant]}
  :action      {:theme :author-data
                :purpose "Apply a named Action Type with args; ALWAYS previews a dry-run plan first, then asks to apply."
                :when "The in-platform writeback path. Prefer an Action over ad-hoc create/update when the change is a named, repeatable operation."
                :patterns
                ["Read the dry-run plan before confirming — it shows exactly which objects change."
                 "Encode business rules as the action's submission criteria (user/parameter conditions), not in the caller — the action only applies when they all pass."]
                :examples ["action dedup-locations --plan"]
                :see-also [:run-function :action]}
  :run-function {:theme :author-data
                 :purpose "Call a Function by name, `function run <name> param=value…`. A QUERY function answers its rows read-only (the same run an app's `function!` makes, without the app's scope); an EDIT function computes and applies its own Ontology edits — standalone, the authoring/helper path. The server tells which, from the function's declared kind. Arguments bind the function's parameters by name and arrive as strings."
                 :examples ["function run reschedulable-aircraft"
                            "function run aircraft-by-type typ=A320"]
                 :when "While WRITING the function — to see what a query function returns, or what an edit function would do. To ship it to users, back an Action Type with it: an action whose only rule is `{:op :apply-edit-function :function <name>}`. Only then does the edit get typed parameters, submission criteria, permissions, the action log and branch side-effect suppression — `function run` has none of that."
                 :patterns
                 ["Reach for an edit Function when the edit is beyond plain create/modify/delete rules — the function then owns the whole edit."
                  "A function-backed action must declare its `:edits` provenance (which object/link types the function may touch); the run is REFUSED if it edits outside it, since nothing can be read off the rule statically."
                  "`:inputs` maps the function's parameters onto action parameters; omit it and each function parameter is taken from the identically-named action parameter."
                  "Publish the function at a `:version` (SemVer) and the Action can PIN it: `{:op :apply-edit-function :function F :version \"1.2.0\"}` runs that release whatever the source has become since. Add `:auto-upgrade true` to follow later backward-compatible releases. A published version is IMMUTABLE — re-pushing it with different code is refused, and so is a signature break released as a minor/patch version."
                  "When the action fires over many objects at once (an automation over every match), let the function take the WHOLE batch in one execution: `:batched true` plus `:inputs` naming the struct's fields. Its single parameter then receives a list of structs, one entry per call — one execution instead of N, and the only way the function can see conflicts BETWEEN the calls."
                  "Reads inside an edit function see the ontology as it was BEFORE the run — a search after a create does not find the new object. Declare `:staged-writes true` on the function for read-after-write: every search, traversal, aggregation and nested function or Action in that execution then sees what it staged so far, nobody else does, and it all commits once at the end — or not at all if anything throws."
                  "A run is bounded: `:timeout-ms` on the function (default 60000, allowed 1000–600000) is a DEADLINE over the whole evaluation — each model call gets only what is left — and past it the run is discarded, nothing it staged lands. It may load at most 100,000 objects and apply at most 10,000 edits; a model conversation at most 800,000 characters. An Action backed by the function inherits its limits."
                  "`:snapshot` on the function: `:default` gives every read of a run ONE ontology snapshot — unless the run reaches an external system (`:use-llm`, a webhook, a Python query function), which then reads live. `:disabled` reads live always. `:enabled` keeps the snapshot even across a model or webhook call — that holds a database connection for the whole call, so it is bounded by the time limit and by a small instance-wide number of such holders (SUTRA_SNAPSHOT_HOLDERS_MAX, default 4); reach for it only when the run must not see data move mid-run."]
                 :anti-patterns
                 [{:rule "Don't combine a function rule with other Ontology rules in one action."
                   :why "A function rule must be the action's only rule: the function owns the whole edit."}
                  {:rule "Don't expose an edit Function to users via `function run`."
                   :why "It bypasses the Action-Type seam: no parameters, no submission criteria, no per-action permissions, no action-log row naming the action."}]
                 :see-also [:action]}
  :dataset-write {:theme :author-data :purpose "Append or snapshot rows directly into a dataset."}

  :run-import  {:theme :operate
                :purpose "Materialize data from the configured sources/transforms (a build). Scope it with `--only <targets>`; a target is an object-type, a transform, or a DATASET."
                :when "After sources/transforms are installed + committed, to pull/derive the actual data. To pull an EXTERNAL source (IMAP/REST) fresh, this — not a downstream target build — is the trigger."
                :patterns
                ["A job has no time limit unless its transform states one: `@configure(allowed_run_duration=timedelta(hours=2))` (`from sutra_transforms import configure`). Preview, unit tests and Functions keep their limits."
                 "A DATASET is a first-class build target: `run-import --only raw_extract` builds the dataset-terminal transform that produces it and nothing else — no downstream object-type has to be materialized to fill it. That is what makes an expensive LLM extract worth splitting from cheap deterministic shaping: build the checkpoint, eyeball it (`dataset-preview` / `transactions`), then re-run the shaping for free. A dataset build publishes 0 objects and 0 links by design — read the `scope:` line (targets → jobs planned), not the counts."
                 "opt-in, never the default); `between --from <upstream>` = only the transforms on the path from that boundary down to the targets, with the boundary itself treated as an already-built INPUT (re-shape a persisted checkpoint with no re-Grok); `selected` = exactly the named nodes, nothing upstream (dependencies AMONG them are still ordered). Omit it for the default: the targets + their intermediate (t_) chain, which is what --only has always done."
                 "A re-import's cost is storage/publish, not the incrementally-skipped LLM transforms — scope with :only when iterating."
                 "`--profile` answers where THIS run's time went, without arming anything and whatever else the process does: `build show <id>` carries `progress.profile` while it runs and after it ended, also after a failure: threads' time, CPU against waiting, the busiest frames, inclusive time per sutra function and the run's SQL shapes. The SQL tracer is borrowed for it, so its stack capture is part of the numbers (`inflight/stack`). `build show` lists time in the queue as the phase `queued`; `build jobs` names a slow read before the code as the span `input-sigs`."
                 "A bare `run-import` (no :only) is REFUSED — it would re-pull every source and re-run every transform. An external pull (IMAP/REST/sync) has no input, so the planner never sees it as 'stale': a build scoped to a DOWNSTREAM object-type only re-maps the already-landed raw data and never re-pulls. To pull fresh, name the pull itself — its transform or its landing dataset: `run-import --only <pull_transform>`."
                 "`--trace` prints a per-transform READ-PATH diagnostic: which input rows each Python transform received (bootstrap full-read vs. added-delta vs. non-incremental, with the low..high snapshot boundary + row count) and how many rows previous_output() covered. Use it to debug an incremental-SKIP (a transform processing 0 rows): `delta-added rows=0 done=N` = empty added-delta (pointer/boundary excludes the rows); `bootstrap-full-read rows=N done=N` = full input the done-filter swallowed. The trace also echoes to the backend stdout log, so it survives a long build that 504's."
                 "`--trace` ALSO prints a per-transform CODE-EXECUTION diagnostic (`trace (code execution)`, emitted by the python subprocess and the clojure interpreter alike): what the sandbox DID with its input — `chat-calls=M` model calls it made (0 over a non-empty input = the transform's OWN done-filter swallowed every row) plus a content-SAFE data FINGERPRINT of each input and of previous_output: `rows=N cols=[…] key=<pk> key-nulls=N sample=[…] null-cols=[…]`. The fingerprint answers the `chat-calls=0` follow-up WITHOUT hand-querying the DB: `key-nulls` = how many rows lack the incremental key (all-null ⇒ the done-filter matched on a broken/absent key); `cols` shows whether the input even carries the key column (it may be renamed — input `cols=[tid …]` vs previous_output `key=pk` means the dedup keys differ); `sample` (the KEY column only — an id, never body text) shows whether the input keys actually OVERLAP previous_output. Author code can drop its own breadcrumbs with `from sutra_transforms import trace; trace(\"kept\", rows=n)` (python) or `(sutra/trace \"kept\" :rows n)` (clojure) — a no-op unless --trace is set."
                 "Staleness: a job whose logic and inputs are unchanged since its last COMPLETED run is FRESH and not recomputed — `jobs <id>` shows it DID_NOT_RUN with `fresh — … since run <id>`. Jobs reading an external source, an external system or a derived property are never fresh. `--force` recomputes every job in scope anyway; `--full` additionally ignores incrementality and writes a SNAPSHOT."
                 "A failed job aborts only its dependents; every other job COMPLETES and keeps what it wrote — its transactions commit and its objects publish — and the build still ends FAILED. A cycle or a missing input fails the build before any job starts (400, no run)."
                 "default 0) before each attempt; its downstream jobs stay WAITING until the verdict is final. Only environment faults are retried — worker/transport unreachable, 429/502/503/504, a dropped or serialization-failed database connection. A transform's own exception, a data-restriction rejection and a cancellation are final at once: the inputs are pinned within a build, so a second attempt could only repeat them. `--abort-on-failure` cancels every other WAITING/RUNNING job (ABORTED) as soon as one job's failure is final; without it only the failed job's dependents are aborted and independent jobs run on. All three land on the run row — `run <id>` shows them, `jobs <id>` shows each job's `attempt`."]
                :anti-patterns
                [{:rule "Don't force a full rebuild of everything just to refresh one output."
                  :why "Force builds re-run transforms whose inputs are unchanged, wasting compute; scope the build and reserve force-refresh for the source sync itself."}
                 {:rule
                  "Don't pause schedules or wait for `build list` to go idle before a build."
                  :why "Builds lock what they write: a build that reads or writes what a running build writes is QUEUED — `watch` shows `… queued behind <id>` — and starts when that one ends; non-overlapping builds run side by side (up to `settings build-concurrency`, default 4). `build stop <id>` takes a queued build out of the queue."}
                 {:rule "Don't expect a full `run-import` to re-charge the LLM for already-processed rows."
                  :why "Incremental transforms skip rows already in their own output (dedup by their output key via previous_output), so a full re-import re-runs the cheap SQL/map steps but Groks only genuinely-new rows — see the incremental-sync concept."}]
                :examples ["run-import --only pull_mailbox"
                           "run-import --only classify_events,derive_partners"
                           "run-import --only raw_extract"
                           "run-import --only bestellung --strategy ancestors"
                           "run-import --only shape_positionen --strategy between --from raw_extract"
                           "run-import --only raw_extract,shape_positionen --strategy selected"
                           "run-import --only classify_events --trace"
                           "run-import --only pull_mailbox --retry-count 3 --retry-backoff 1m --abort-on-failure"]
                :see-also [:watch :runs :jobs :lineage :schedules :incremental-sync :force-snapshot
                           :build-estimate]}
  :build-estimate {:theme :operate
                   :purpose "The LLM Kostenvoranschlag: what the model calls of THAT build would cost, before it runs. Takes `build run`'s scope flags verbatim (--only/--strategy/--from) and plans the same scope, then prices each language-model transform in it — TWO numbers, because they are two questions: what pressing run ONCE costs (bounded by the transform's per-build cap) and what draining the whole backlog costs, over N builds. Plus the verdict against today's remaining daily budget. Read-only: nothing runs, no model is called."
                   :when "Before an expensive or unfamiliar build, before a first build after a prompt/model change, and whenever you want to know whether the daily budget will bite before you spend it finding out. Also the honest answer to \"how much is still to do?\" — OFFEN is the backlog, not the table size."
                   :patterns
                   ["OFFEN is what is LEFT, not what is read — provided the transform declares its drain: `@transform(done_key=\"<input>.<column>\", max_per_run=N)` (python) or `:done-key {:input :x :key :c}` + `:max-per-run N` (EDN). The estimate then counts the ANTI-JOIN between that input column and the transform's own output primary key, which is exactly what `previous_output()` filters on, and bounds one build by the cap. Without the declaration the column falls back to the consumer-pointer DELTA — rows READ — and the GRUNDLAGE column says `Done-Set UNBEKANNT`. Measured on prod: 1851 rows read vs 225 undone, and a cap of 20 per build; that is the difference between the two."
                    "The anti-join is an UPPER BOUND, and GRUNDLAGE says whether you are looking at it. It counts what the SCHEMA can see (keys absent from the output); the filter that decides the real work lives in the python body (`if not is_purchasing(mail): continue`), and the platform cannot read a predicate without running it. Measured on prod: 1641 by the anti-join vs 225 through the gate (×7), 898 vs 21 (×43). The cure needs no declaration — `cap_batch(pending)` COUNTS what it was handed, the number lands on the Job row as `pending_observed`, and the next estimate prints `gemessen 225 · Obergrenze 1641`. A transform that has not capped yet shows the ceiling and says so (`noch ohne Messung`)."
                    "`prompt_key` is the sharper skip-key: `@transform(prompt_key=[\"mail.subject\", \"mail.body_text\"])` (python) or `:prompt-key {:input :mail :columns [:subject :body-text] :column :prompt-hash}` (EDN). Done then means \"this QUESTION was already asked\", not \"a row with this id exists\": the body filters with `unprocessed(rows, previous_output())` and stamps `prompt_hash(row)` into each output row, and the estimate anti-joins the identical md5 in SQL. A changed prompt input is pending again, and a `semantic_version` bump invalidates every key by construction — no `write_mode='replace'` run needed to retire the answers the old prompt gave. The output object-type MUST declare the stamp attribute; if the two sides' hashes never meet over a non-empty output, the estimate raises it as a mismatch instead of reporting the whole table as backlog."
                    "GRUNDLAGE also says how the price per call was obtained: `measured N calls/row` (tokens AND fan-out from the last --days of llm_call_log), `1 call je offener Zeile` (the drain tier — one pending item is one call, no fan-out guess needed), `per-call` (tokens measured, 1 call/row assumed) or `uncalibrated` (never ran through the model service)."
                    "An uncalibrated transform costs `unknown`, NEVER 0, and the total is then printed as `≥` — a lower bound. Treat the headline as the floor, not the price."
                    "Over budget is not a build failure, which is why it is worth knowing in advance: the guard REFUSES each call past the cap, the build stays GREEN, and the rows it skipped are carried forward with no results. The estimate names the transform and the row where the cap would bite."
                    "The invoice for the same build afterwards is `llm cost --run <run-id>` — same money formula, calls that actually happened."]
                   :examples ["build estimate --only rechnung"
                              "build estimate --only bestellung --strategy ancestors"
                              "build estimate --only classify_events --days 7"
                              "build estimate --only parse_positions --calls-per-row 4"
                              "build estimate --json"]
                   :see-also [:run-import :llm :watch :delta :preview-transform]}
  :force-snapshot {:theme :operate
                   :purpose "Queues a snapshot: the next build of the transform writing this dataset reads its inputs in full and replaces its outputs. Nothing changes until that build, the transaction history included. Its consumers re-read the replaced dataset by themselves."
                   :when "To rebuild an incremental output from scratch — e.g. re-ingest an external pull from its :since floor. Follow with `run-import`; the job runs even if its inputs and logic are unchanged. A failed build keeps the request."
                   :anti-patterns
                   [{:rule "Don't force-snapshot after you CHANGED A TRANSFORM'S CODE/MODEL/PROMPT."
                     :why "That is what a :semantic-version bump states, and it stays true for every branch the code reaches. See incremental-sync."}
                    {:rule "Don't expect it on a dataset no transform writes."
                     :why "It is refused: no build would ever serve it. Write a snapshot with `dataset write` instead."}]
                   :examples ["force-snapshot raw_messages"
                              "force-snapshot raw_messages --message reset-cursor"]
                   :see-also [:run-import :transactions :incremental-sync]}
  :reindex {:theme :operate
            :purpose "Unlike a build's reconcile it ALWAYS propagates an authoritative empty (overrides the flaky-empty valve), so it corrects the exact drift `health` flags as `index-drift:<type>`."
            :when "After `health` reports `index-drift:<type>` — stale object instances outlive their source (e.g. a source emptied to zero rows, which the build's reindex conservatively refuses to propagate). Reconciles against whatever the backing table holds NOW, so run `run-import` first if the backing table itself is stale."
            :anti-patterns
            [{:rule "Don't reindex to reprocess a transform after a code/model/prompt change."
              :why "reindex only reconciles the index to the backing table as-is; it does not re-run the transform. Bump :semantic-version for that (see incremental-sync)."}
             {:rule "Don't reindex an object type with no backing transform (Action/function-created)."
              :why "There is no authoritative t_<name> to reconcile against; the verb reports no backing transform."}
             {:rule "Don't reach for `--force` on a source-backed type that also carries WRITEBACK-only objects."
              :why "The verb reconciles AUTHORITATIVELY (unlike a build, which passes authoritative?=false for source-fed types and so never deletes writeback rows) — so objects created by an Action/automation and absent from the source read as orphans. It once deleted 10 llm-discovered `lieferant` rows in one call, reported as '10 orphaned instances entfernt'. Since 2026-07-24 named actions record `object_edits` provenance and are exempt, and the verb PREVIEWS the deletions and returns 409 unless `--force` — which is exactly the guard `--force` removes. Read the count and sample it names before overriding it."}]
            :examples ["reindex monatswert" "reindex email"]
            :see-also [:health :force-snapshot :run-import]}
  :prune-orphans {:theme :operate
                  :purpose "physical tables no live entity claims, left behind when an entity was delete+recreated or its RID re-minted. Always PREVIEWS first (each table + its row count) and asks before dropping; `--yes` skips the prompt. Drops on the working branch and on a live staging branch, each judged against its OWN schema. Recoverable through the schema history (AS OF)."
                  :when "`check` reports :orphan-tables and you want the disk back, or you want a clean integrity report. NOT urgent: an orphan table breaks nothing — no read path touches it, no FK points at it, and (since the gate demotion) it does not block commits."
                  :anti-patterns
                  [{:rule "Don't run prune-orphans to fix a 'table not found' read error."
                    :why "That is the INVERSE class (:tableless-types — a schema row with no table), which self-heals at commit. Pruning drops tables; it cannot create one."}
                   {:rule "Don't prune without reading the row counts in the preview."
                    :why "A non-zero count is real data. Confirm the live replacement entity already holds those rows (e.g. compare edge counts for a re-minted link) before dropping."}]
                  :examples ["ontology prune-orphans" "ontology prune-orphans --yes"]
                  :see-also [:check :ontology-lint :reindex]}
  :prune-resolution
  {:theme :operate
   :purpose "cleanup: drop the entity-resolution crosswalk and provenance rows no still-resolved object-type claims"
   :when "After a type stops being a resolved entity (`:resolve-entity`/`:match-keys` removed). Nothing else reclaims this state: `prune-orphans` sweeps TABLES and `drop-branch!` sweeps a branch's history, while these two tables are keyed by object-type and entity."
   :anti-patterns [{:rule "Don't reach for raw SQL against control_entity_crosswalk."
                    :why "This verb derives the survivors from the live schema with the importer's own `resolved-object-type?` — `:resolve-entity` AND non-empty `:match-keys`, both halves — so it cannot delete state a resolver still uses. A hand-written DELETE has no such guard, and the tables carry no column that would tell you."}
                   {:rule "Don't read a large :elsewhere count as work left undone."
                    :why "Whether a type still resolves is a fact about ONE branch's schema, and the verb holds only the branch it runs on. Other branches are reported so nothing is hidden, never swept on this branch's evidence — check out that branch and run it there."}]
   :examples ["ontology prune-resolution" "ontology prune-resolution --apply"
              "ontology prune-resolution --apply --yes"]
   :see-also [:prune-orphans :media-gc]}
  :media-gc {:theme :operate
             :purpose "Reclaim CAS bytes nothing references any more — the counterpart the content-addressed blob store never had. REFERENCED = every :media-reference / [:array :media-reference] value on EVERY branch, plus the :blob catalog rows, plus every hash the git projection wrote through (control_repo_files / control_git_commits — that write-through is what makes $SUTRA_GIT_DIR a rebuildable cache). Everything else, older than --grace-days (default 7), is reclaimable. REPORTS by default; --apply deletes after a confirm (--yes skips it)."
             :when "The blob volume (/var/sutra/blobs) has grown and you want to know what of it is still load-bearing — e.g. after deleting a media-set dataset, retiring an upload pipeline, or re-uploading a corrected file under a new key. Run bare first; the report alone answers 'how much of this is garbage'."
             :anti-patterns [{:rule "Don't treat a blocked sweep as 'nothing to clean'."
                              :why "`:blocked :incomplete-scan` means a branch or dataset could not be read — what was not read cannot be called unreferenced. Fix the read (or drop the dead branch) and run again; the refusal is the safety."}
                             {:rule "Don't lower --grace-days to 0 to reclaim more."
                              :why "An upload lands bytes first and the row a moment later. A zero grace window can sweep a blob between those two steps, leaving a row that points at nothing — the one failure the CAS cannot repair on its own."}
                             {:rule "Don't run it as a fix for a missing document in an app."
                              :why "A blob a media-reference column names is referenced by definition and is never swept. A missing document is a broken reference (the row lost its hash), which this verb reports but does not create."}]
             :examples ["media gc" "media gc --grace-days 30" "media gc --apply --yes"]
             :see-also [:prune-orphans :blob :upload]}
  :retention-plan
  {:theme :operate
   :purpose "Retention over the datasets main declares: per dataset with a transaction log, its HORIZON now (no :at read or delta below it is answered), the horizon the policies give, how many transactions that retires and which policies do. Policies combine like this: a transaction goes when ANY policy selects it, a policy selects when ALL its selectors do, and none touches the latest view (from the last SNAPSHOT on) unless it allows that. Read-only."
   :when "The pg database grows, or you want to know how far back `:at`/`delta`/restore still reach for a dataset."
   :examples ["retention plan" "retention plan av_kre_raw"]
   :see-also [:retention-run :retention-policies :transactions]}
  :retention-run
  {:theme :operate
   :purpose "Mark and sweep: MARK each proposed horizon (reads below it are refused from now on, the data stays), then SWEEP every mark at least --grace-days old (default 7): history partitions wholly below it dropped, large ones compacted, the rest trimmed. The scheduled retention job does the same once an hour with the default grace. PREVIEWS by default — `--apply` to act."
   :when "After setting a policy, to mark at once instead of within the hour; or with --grace-days 0 when the space is needed now and nobody needs the marked versions."
   :anti-patterns [{:rule "Don't use --grace-days 0 as the routine."
                    :why "The grace is the window in which a wrong mark can still be taken back (delete the policy, the data is still there). After the sweep the versions are gone for good."}]
   :examples ["retention run" "retention run --apply" "retention run av_kre_raw --apply --grace-days 0"]
   :see-also [:retention-plan :retention-policies]}
  :retention-policies
  {:theme :operate
   :purpose "The dataset retention policies: the RECOMMENDED ones (the platform's, not editable: keep the last 10 views; a version superseded more than 30 days ago goes in any view) and the CUSTOM ones (`retention policy set`)."
   :examples ["retention policies"]
   :see-also [:retention-policy-set :retention-plan]}
  :retention-policy-set
  {:theme :operate
   :purpose "Create or replace a custom retention policy. Selectors (all must hold): --older-than-days N, --view-count N (keep the last N views), --transaction-count N (keep the N most recent data-containing transactions), --views-older-than-days N (its view closed more than N days ago). --datasets all or a comma list of dataset names/rids. --latest-view lets it delete from the latest view — superseded row versions only; the dataset as it is never goes."
   :when "A dataset whose writes come so often that the recommended policies keep too much — e.g. a full-refresh sync every ten minutes: `--view-count 3` for it."
   :anti-patterns [{:rule "Don't expect a custom policy to keep MORE than the recommended ones."
                    :why "Policies combine with OR: a custom policy can only retire more."}]
   :examples ["retention policy set raw-syncs --datasets av_kre_raw --view-count 3"]
   :see-also [:retention-policies :retention-plan :retention-policy-delete]}
  :retention-policy-delete
  {:theme :operate
   :purpose "Remove a custom retention policy. Marks it already made stay; a mark younger than the grace period still has its data."
   :examples ["retention policy delete raw-syncs"]
   :see-also [:retention-policies]}
  :schema-code-blobs
  {:theme :operate
   :purpose "One-shot migration of the schema tables onto the storage form they already write. Two moves in one door: authored SOURCE (transform :code, application/function :source, every published function release) leaves the schema map for the blob CAS behind a content address; and each history row's WORLD SNAPSHOT becomes one row per entity that write CHANGED. REPORTS by default — and the dry run is the only honest answer to how much there is to reclaim, since no figure is written down anywhere."
   :when "Once, after the deploy that introduced the new form. Everything written since then is already migrated; this is for the rows that predate it. Run bare first — the report costs nothing and tells you the size of the job."
   :anti-patterns [{:rule "Don't run it expecting the tables to shrink on disk."
                    :why "Postgres does not return space after an UPDATE. The rows get smaller; `pg_total_relation_size` does not move until a VACUUM FULL, which takes an exclusive lock and is a separate decision."}
                   {:rule "Don't run --apply during a build or with schedules armed."
                    :why "It rewrites `control_schema_history` row by row. Nothing breaks if it is interrupted — a row is readable whether or not it has been converted — but a build reading the schema while the history is half-converted is work you cannot attribute."}
                   {:rule "Don't treat it as a cleanup you can repeat for more space."
                    :why "It is idempotent: the second run finds nothing. If it reports nothing and you expected something, the rows were already migrated — not skipped."}]
   :examples ["schema code-blobs" "schema code-blobs --apply" "schema code-blobs --apply --yes"]
   :see-also [:media-gc :schema-history]}
  :edit-history {:theme :inspect
                 :purpose "The user-edit history for ONE object type. Two aligned views: the edit-TRANSACTION chain (one typed transaction per consumer Action-submit, recorded against the object-type's own RID — which `transactions` never shows, since for an object type it redirects to the PRODUCER's dataset chain) and the per-ROW `object_edits` provenance overlay (current edit_kind create/update/delete per primary key). Read-only."
                 :when "To see who edited an object type and how, after consumer apps submit Actions — the audit companion to `transactions` (which only shows the datasource/build chain, never user edits)."
                 :examples ["edit-history vorhaben" "edit-history kostenaufteilung --limit 100"]
                 :see-also [:transactions :drop-edits :audit]}
  :drop-edits {:theme :operate
               :purpose "Clears the `object_edits` provenance (so reconcile no longer exempts the rows) then reindexes — user-CREATED rows absent from the source are swept. DESTRUCTIVE and irreversible."
               :when "To reset an object type's user edits — e.g. after testing edits on a type before releasing it, or to re-establish 'the datasource is the truth'. Property-value edits on still-sourced rows revert on the next build (the import upsert re-writes source values)."
               :anti-patterns
               [{:rule
                 "Don't drop-edits to fix stale index drift — use `reindex` (non-destructive)."
                 :why "drop-edits deletes user edit provenance and sweeps user-created rows; reindex only reconciles the index to the backing table without touching edits."}]
               :examples ["drop-edits vorhaben"]
               :see-also [:edit-history :reindex]}
  :rename {:theme :operate
           :purpose "Rename a schema entity IN PLACE (RID-stable): `rename <from> <to> [--kind <k>]`. The entity keeps its RID, so every resource ref, its project ownership (`:project`) and a project's grants survive — only the name field changes. Default `--kind` is :project (the one rename with no other CLI surface); also handles :object-type / :link-type / :interface / :function / :dataset / :object-type-group / :application (that one reads better as `app rename <from> <to>`)."
           :when "You want a cleaner name for a project/type/link without re-creating it and re-pointing everything. Commits immediately (like the other rename handlers), not staged."
           :anti-patterns [{:rule "Don't use rename to MOVE a resource into a different project."
                            :why "rename changes an entity's own name, not its owner. To set ownership, install the resource with `:project <name>` (or the update-<kind> action) — see the :project field."}
                           {:rule "Don't rename an APP by `git mv`ing its `apps/<name>.enta` (+ the `.cljs`) and editing `:name`."
                            :why "That lands two apps, not one moved app. A spec is omit-preserved — absence from a git tree retires a TYPE, not an application — so the old app stays PUBLISHED and the projection writes its files back on the next install, while the edited file installs a SECOND app under a fresh RID that no grant, kompass placement or scenario branch points at. `app rename` flips the name field in place, and the projected file set (`apps/<name>.enta`, the `:source-file` sibling, the pulled `exports/<name>.<ext>`) is derived from that field, so it moves with the rename."}]
           :examples ["rename ersatzteile-portal ersatzteile" "rename werft-portal werft"
                      "rename altname neuname --kind object-type"
                      "app rename wochenvorschau schichtplanung"]
           :see-also [:projects :grant :apps]}
  :branch-pipeline {:theme :version
                    :purpose "BRANCH the pipeline: `branch-pipeline <seed> [label] [--from <parent>]`. Creates a durable branch carrying the SAME pipeline (no rename) and checks it out, so you can edit its transforms in isolation and merge back. Composes the existing branch-create + checkout; the branch name encodes the pipeline (`pipeline/<seed>[/<label>]`) for provenance."
                    :when "You want to experiment on a pipeline safely and possibly merge back to the parent (default `main`), rather than making a permanent copy. Edit the transforms on the branch, build there, then merge back."
                    :examples ["branch-pipeline plan-ergebnis"
                               "branch-pipeline plan-ergebnis rounding-fix"
                               "branch-pipeline plan-ergebnis wip --from main"]
                    :see-also [:branch :checkout]}
  :delete-construct {:theme :author-schema
                     :purpose "Delete ONE named schema construct by kind — object-type / link-type / transform / function / dataset / action-type — via that kind's dedicated DELETE endpoint. Drops the TYPE, never object instances (those change through an authored Action Type). Immediate (not staged); reversible via the branch."
                     :when "Removing a construct from the ontology. The `:kind` rides on the noun path: `ontology object-type delete <name>`, `ontology link-type delete <name>`, `ontology action-type delete <name>`, `transform delete <name>`, `dataset delete <name>`. A refused delete NAMES its referrers, and an authored action type is a common one — retire it here before the type it references. `--cascade` takes an object type's link types along, and on a dataset unbinds the `:backing` of every object type it backs (the types keep their objects; nothing fills them any more)."
                     :see-also [:rename :dependents]}
  :set-status {:theme :author-schema
               :purpose "Set the lifecycle :status of one object-type / link-type / interface, with an optional deprecation sidecar (--reason/--deadline/--replacement). A TARGETED in-place patch — never rebuilds the spec, so :attributes survive (unlike a full update)."
               :when "Governing the delete guard: an :active/:endorsed type cannot be deleted, so promote a stable type to :active to protect it, or set :deprecated (with a reason/deadline) to signal + then delete. The `:kind` rides on the noun path: `ontology object-type status <name> <status>`."
               :see-also [:delete-construct :describe :dependents]}
  :trash {:theme :author-schema
          :purpose "The recoverable-deletions view: constructs deleted from the branch but still present in recent schema history. In Sutra-OS delete IS soft — the store is git-for-data, so `delete-construct` leaves the pre-delete spec in the commit graph (no bespoke trash table). Shows the most recent commit where each deleted construct still existed."
          :when "After a delete you regret, or to see what's recoverable before purging history. Restore with `project restore <kind> <name>`."
          :see-also [:restore :delete-construct]}
  :restore {:theme :author-schema
            :purpose "Undelete ONE construct (object-type / link-type / transform / function) by kind + name: re-adds its most-recent historical spec at its ORIGINAL rid — so resource refs, project ownership and grants survive — and re-runs the DDL. Pure history recovery; no snapshot table."
            :when "Recovering a construct listed by `project trash`. 404 if it's not in recent history; 400 if it already exists."
            :examples ["restore transform classify-events" "restore object-type bestellung"]
            :see-also [:trash :delete-construct]}
  :edit {:theme :code
         :purpose "Open ONE resource's editable body in $EDITOR — read RESOURCE-scoped from the live schema, edit, then stage a partial install of just that edit and gate review→commit. The `:kind` rides on the noun path: `transform edit` / `app edit` (CODE), or `ontology object-type edit` (its author .ent fragment — Ontology Editor, structural)."
         :when "Iterating on a transform's logic (the LLM classify loop: edit the tool prompt, re-run, diff) or reshaping an object-type's fields/title/status. The direct-manipulation edit→Review→Save loop; non-destructive — only the named resource is reconciled."
         :patterns
         ["python transforms write IN PLACE (they round-trip through :code alone — the @transform decorator re-infers inputs/output at install)."
          "clojure transforms write IN PLACE too, but their whole authored entry is re-emitted with the new body swapped in: they carry EDN :inputs/:output that nothing can re-infer, so saving the code alone would strip the wiring."
          "`ontology object-type edit` opens the type's author fragment (:attributes/:title/:status/:implements) as EDN; re-install reconciles + runs the DDL. An :external-source-backed (synced) type is read-only here — editing would drop the sync binding."
          "SQL transforms and app views are READ-ONLY here (`--print`): their resolved bindings need the whole-project CODE checkout — edit those via `clone` → `push`."
          "`--print` dumps the current body (pipe it); `--from <file>` applies an already-edited file with no TTY (scriptable); bare invocation spawns $EDITOR."
          "This is also the door to a SOURCE SYNC's SELECT: `transform edit <dataset>-sync --print` / `--from <file.sql>`. An `:external-source` binding is LIFTED at install into that `<name>-sync` transform and STRIPPED off the dataset spec, so the query survives only there, under `:source :query`. Editing re-emits the author fragment and the install door re-lifts it — a clean 2-action reconcile. Only the source CREDENTIALS are sealed; the query is freely editable."]
         :anti-patterns [{:rule "Don't expect a widened sync query to backfill the new columns."
                          :why "The sync is INCREMENTAL and watermarks ROWS, not schema — so after committing the query change, `build run --only <type>` re-shapes off the OLD raw and the new columns land all-null. Force the re-pull: `dataset snapshot <raw>` (resets incremental, leaves the raw briefly EMPTY) → `build run --only <raw> --strategy ancestors` (only `ancestors` on the raw runs the producing egress sync; `--only <dataset>` alone or `--strategy selected` does not) → `build run --only <type> --strategy ancestors`. Verify each hop with `dataset preview <raw>`, then `query <type>`."}
                         {:rule "Don't try to edit a SQL transform or an app view in place."
                          :why "Their resolved bindings need the whole-project CODE checkout, so the verb refuses with 'in-place edit of this resource isn't supported yet'. Reach them through `projection pull code-repository` → edit → `projection push`. A transform that sits in NO repository has to be filed into one first (`repository place <repo> <transform>`), and on a protected trunk that placement is itself a proposal."}]
         :examples ["transform edit classify-events --print"
                    "ontology object-type edit bestellung --print"
                    "transform edit classify-events --from /tmp/classify.py"]
         :see-also [:preview-transform :run :describe :review :save]}
  :automations {:theme :operate
                :purpose "Declare / upsert automation rules: object-data condition → effect. `:effect {:action A :args {…}}` submits an Action per object; `{:function F :inputs {…}}` runs an edit Function per object. `:mode :propose` on either stages each Action as an agent proposal for human review instead of applying it. A propose-mode Action effect can REPLAY a recorded reading (an LLM output already stored on the objects): `:key` (one proposal per reading, never staged twice — decided or not), `:reason` (text or `{:template \"… {{prop}} …\"}`) and `:provenance {:model … :read-at … :source-dataset … :source-pk … :prompt-hash …}`, each evaluated per object like `:args`; an object parameter the reading names by primary key takes `{:ref <type> :pk {:self <prop>}}` (resolved to the object id when staged; no single match = left open); missing values stay open for the reviewer instead of holding the reading back. Run such a rule once by hand (`automation run`), without `:every-ms`."
                :patterns
                ["Design the rule for bulk: one single-execution effect over the whole matched set with an action that takes an object-set parameter; cap high-churn object conditions with a time condition."]
                :anti-patterns
                [{:rule
                  "Don't add an automation just to set a property to a fixed or computed value."
                  :why "That is an Action, not an automation — invoke the action directly on the target object set for more control and less compute."}]
                :see-also [:automation :automate]}
  :automation  {:theme :operate
                :purpose "Control one automation rule: status / run / preview / pause / unpause / delete / runs, and review what a `:mode :propose` rule staged: proposals / accept / reject (accept submits the proposed Action as YOU; `param=value` after the id edits the proposal first)."
                :patterns
                ["Preview or run a rule once before unpausing it, and watch its execution frequency — a rule firing many times a day (or chaining into others) is the classic runaway-cost signal."
                 "Backfill after a new function version = manual execution: `--do run` with `--ids a,b` and/or `--where '<:when EDN>'` runs the effect for every object of that set — :when and :once aside, a done object runs again — in batches (`--batch-size`, default 100; `--parallelism`). The execution is a record the leader runs batch by batch, so a deploy loses nothing (the next leader goes on, a batch is never run twice); it answers the execution id, `--do executions` shows how far each is, `--do runs` each batch, `automation <name> <execution-id> --do cancel` stops one. Name the set narrowly: each object of a Logic effect is one model call."]
                :examples ["automation my-rule --do status" "automation my-rule --do run"
                           "automation belege-lesen --do run --ids <object-id>"
                           "automation belege-lesen --do run --where '[:= [:get :gelesen-art] \"mahnung\"]' --batch-size 50"
                           "automation proposals my-rule --status open --limit 50"
                           "automation accept my-rule <proposal-id>"
                           "automation accept my-rule <proposal-id> betrag=118.40"]}
  :automation-list {:theme :operate
                    :purpose "List every automation rule on the branch: its trigger (:on), cadence, paused state and last-fired time — the read `automation <name> --do status` never gives you across ALL rules."
                    :see-also [:automation :automations]}
  :schedule-list   {:theme :operate
                    :purpose "List every build schedule on the branch: target, cadence (cron / every-ms), failure handling (retries, backoff, abort-on-failure), paused state and last-fired time. the observability read `schedule <name> --do status` needs a name you already know."
                    :see-also [:schedule :schedules]}
  :schedule-candidates {:theme :operate
                        :purpose "The DAG-derived parent nodes a `:parent-updated` trigger on <target> can watch. Lists the object-type/dataset inputs one hop upstream of <target> (a source-fed/root node has none ⇒ use a cadence, not a parent trigger)."
                        :patterns
                        ["The event trigger propagates an ingest downstream WITHOUT a whole-ontology rebuild: each stage watches its parent and builds only its own scoped target."]
                        :examples ["schedule candidates email" "schedule candidates lead"]
                        :see-also [:schedule-create :lineage :schedule-list]}
  :schedule-create {:theme :operate
                    :purpose "Create/upsert one build schedule (by name): `--target T` scopes the build; `--on-parent-update P[,P…]` makes it a `:parent-updated` EVENT trigger (fire when P's data changes); `--scope self|with-dependencies|downstream|connecting`; `--from B` gives connecting's upstream boundary; `--cron C` / `--every-ms N` for a TIME cadence (OR-combined with the event). No trigger and no cadence ⇒ it never fires (warned)."
                    :patterns ["not one whole-graph rebuild."
                               "Discover the parent first with `schedule candidates <target>`; pair connecting with `--on-parent-update <B>` so the subpipeline refreshes exactly when its boundary changes."
                               "`--retry-count N --retry-backoff <dur> --abort-on-failure` are the build options every firing of this schedule passes on; `schedule list` shows them in ON FAILURE. A re-fire on the next cron tick is a NEW build, not a retry — retries happen inside the build."]
                    :examples
                    ["schedule create refresh-email --target email --on-parent-update raw_messages --scope downstream"
                     "schedule create process-mail --target grok-email-content-extract --scope connecting --from raw_messages --on-parent-update raw_messages"
                     "schedule create pull-mailbox --target raw_messages --cron \"*/15 * * * *\""
                     "schedule create pull-mailbox --target raw_messages --cron \"*/15 * * * *\" --retry-count 3 --retry-backoff 1m --abort-on-failure"]
                    :see-also [:schedule-candidates :schedule :schedule-list :schedules]}
  :linter-list {:theme :operate
                :purpose "What the last sweep recommends: per rule, resource and project, with its reason. A recommendation stays until a later sweep no longer finds it. `linter rules` says why each rule exists."
                :examples ["linter list --mode pipeline-resilience"]
                :see-also [:linter-rules :linter-sweep :linter-snooze]}
  :linter-rules {:theme :operate :purpose "The Linter rules, each with why it exists and what it recommends." :see-also [:linter-list]}
  :linter-sweep {:theme :operate :purpose "Run a sweep now; it also runs daily by itself." :see-also [:linter-list]}
  :linter-snooze {:theme :operate :purpose "Hide a recommendation until a date, with a reason; --off ends the snooze." :see-also [:linter-list]}
  :linter-assign {:theme :operate :purpose "Assign a recommendation to someone for tracking." :see-also [:linter-list]}
  :health-check-list {:theme :operate
                      :purpose "List every declared Data-Health check: kind, bound target, severity, enabled state and its latest result — `health-checks` only upserts them."
                      :see-also [:health-check-run :health :health-checks]}
  :health-check-run  {:theme :operate
                      :purpose "Run ONE Data-Health check by name and print pass/fail (resolves the name to its id client-side). `health` runs ALL checks; this runs a single one."
                      :examples ["health-check-run beleg-freshness"]
                      :see-also [:health :health-check-list]}
  :health-check-set {:theme :operate
                     :purpose "Create or update a Data-Health check in-platform — the resource's health tab: a check is monitoring configuration on a resource, not schema, so it is not branched, reviewed or merged and needs no projection. Without --branch it lands on main."
                     :when "An invariant (identity, row counts, a sum that must agree with a source) should alarm, not just be visible in an app."
                     :patterns
                     ["Flags for one check: `--target <object-type> --kind row-count --min 1 --severity critical`; `--on transform|dataset` binds it to another node kind (a dataset target is its snake_cased name)."
                      "`--from <file.edn>` for the full rider shape (one map or a vector), e.g. `:against` for a reconciliation."]
                     :anti-patterns
                     [{:rule "Don't hard-code `d_<locator>` in a `:custom` query — write `{{object-type}}` / `{{dataset:x}}`."
                       :why "The placeholders resolve the node's physical table at run time and survive renames."}]
                     :examples ["health-check-set beleg-rows --target eingangsbeleg --kind row-count --min 1"
                                "health-check-set --from /abs/path/checks.edn"]
                     :see-also [:health-check-run :health-check-list :health-check-delete]}
  :health-check-delete {:theme :operate
                        :purpose "Remove one Data-Health check by name (the counterpart of health-check-set)."
                        :examples ["health-check-delete beleg-rows"]
                        :see-also [:health-check-set :health-check-list]}
  :health-check-watch {:theme :operate
                       :purpose "Subscribe yourself to one check's failures by name; `--level critical` only for critical ones, `none` stops it. How you are told follows `notifications settings`."
                       :examples ["health-check-watch beleg-rows --level critical"]
                       :see-also [:notification-settings :health-check-snooze]}
  :health-check-snooze {:theme :operate
                        :purpose "Silence one check's alerts for everyone until a time (`--hours N` or `--until <iso>`); the check still runs and records its result."
                        :examples ["health-check-snooze beleg-rows --hours 4 --reason \"Quelle in Wartung\""]
                        :see-also [:health-check-unsnooze :health-check-watch]}
  :health-check-unsnooze {:theme :operate
                          :purpose "End a check's snooze by name, so its alerts go out again."
                          :see-also [:health-check-snooze]}
  :health-check-group-list {:theme :operate
                            :purpose "List the check groups by name: the checks each bundles and who gets its digest."
                            :see-also [:health-check-group-set :health-check-group-subscribe]}
  :health-check-group-set {:theme :operate
                           :purpose "Create or update a check group by name from check names (`--checks a,b,c`); an unknown check name is refused."
                           :examples ["health-check-group-set beleg --checks beleg-rows,beleg-freshness"]
                           :see-also [:health-check-group-list :health-check-group-subscribe]}
  :health-check-group-delete {:theme :operate
                              :purpose "Remove a check group by name, with its subscriptions; the checks stay."
                              :see-also [:health-check-group-list]}
  :health-check-group-subscribe {:theme :operate
                                 :purpose "Get a group's digest yourself, `--every 15m|1h|daily|weekly` (default daily); only when something fails unless `--all`."
                                 :examples ["health-check-group-subscribe beleg --every 1h"]
                                 :see-also [:health-check-group-unsubscribe :notification-settings]}
  :health-check-group-unsubscribe {:theme :operate
                                   :purpose "Stop receiving a group's digest."
                                   :see-also [:health-check-group-subscribe]}
  :notification-settings {:theme :operate
                          :purpose "Show or change how you are told, per kind (action automation review branch mention health default) and way (platform email both off); `--mute`/`--unmute` an automation by id. Without flags it only prints."
                          :examples ["notification-settings --set health=both,default=platform"]
                          :see-also [:health-check-watch :health-check-group-subscribe]}
  :function-list {:theme :ontology
                  :purpose "Merges the `:functions` section with the query-fn transforms (stamped :function-name) — query functions are visible AS functions here, not lost under `transform list`."
                  :see-also [:function-show :function-create :describe]}
  :function-show {:theme :ontology
                  :purpose "Print ONE Function's definition (either lifecycle) — its language, inputs/output (query) or :source/:target-type (edit). Finds it across both the `:functions` section and the query-fn transforms."
                  :examples ["function show active-orders"]
                  :see-also [:function-list :function-create]}
  :agent-list {:theme :llm
               :purpose "List the installed AI Platform agents. Complements the BATCH plane (a :language-model input on a transform/function); `llm usage` shows both together."
               :see-also [:agent-show :agent-run :llm-usage]}
  :agent-show {:theme :llm
               :purpose "Print ONE AI Platform agent's definition — its model, system-prompt, bound tools and retrieval context."
               :examples ["agent show triage"]
               :see-also [:agent-list :agent-run]}
  :agent-run  {:theme :llm
               :purpose "Invoke an AI Platform agent's tool-calling loop over --input text and print its answer (+ per-turn tool trace). The agent may read objects and submit Actions as tools; --max-turns caps the loop."
               :examples ["agent run triage --input \"which invoices are overdue?\""
                          "agent run triage --input \"…\" --max-turns 10"]
               :see-also [:agent-list :agent-show]}
  :new-project {:theme :project
                :purpose "Create a project — an ownership/permission CONTAINER. A schema-mutation construct, so it STAGES through /api/actions (review → commit), then commits unless --stage. Resources reference it via :project; grants on it confer roles across everything it owns."
                :examples ["project create ersatzteile --description \"Spare parts\""
                           "project create scratch --stage"]
                :see-also [:projects :project-map :rename]}
  :new-repository {:theme :code
                   :purpose "Create a Source Repository. A schema-mutation construct like a project, so it STAGES through /api/actions (review → commit), then commits unless --stage. --project names its owning project (a repo belongs to exactly one); --type is transforms (default), functions or applications. This creates the CONTAINER only — put resources in it with `repository place <repo> <resource…>`, which sets each member's :repository back-ref. When SUTRA_GIT_REPOS is on it is a PHYSICAL git repository served at /git/r/<rid>.git, so `clone <project>/<repo>` is a real git clone with real shas, branches and three-way merge."
                   :examples ["repository create etl --project ersatzteile"
                              "repository create fns --project ersatzteile --type functions"
                              "repository create scratch --stage"]
                   :see-also [:repositories :repo-place :new-project :clone :rename]}
  :repo-place {:theme :code
               :purpose "File code-bearing resources INTO a Source Repository — the membership half of `repository create` (which makes only the container). Each resource name resolves to its RID via the live schema and the RID's own kind picks the update action, so one verb covers transforms, functions and applications; an ontology type is refused BY NAME, because types are imported, not committed. A PARTIAL update — it sets only :repository, so nothing else about the resource moves. All placements stage, then ONE commit. Membership is what scopes a repo's checkout: `clone <project>/<repo>` gives you exactly these resources' files, and a push publishes back to exactly these entities."
               :examples ["repository place etl parse-konten derive-konten"
                          "repository place apps werft-vorhaben wochenvorschau"
                          "repository place etl parse-buchungen"]
               :see-also [:new-repository :repositories :clone :place]}
  :repo-unplace {:theme :code
                 :purpose "Take resource(s) OUT of their Source Repository — the inverse of `repository place`, and a PARTIAL update like it: only :repository is cleared, the resource itself is untouched. There was no inverse for a long time, which is a one-way door: a resource could be filed and never unfiled, and the only way back was to edit the schema by hand. It matters most for an Object View, which may not be in a repository at all — a view belongs to its object type and is emitted into the ONTOLOGY tree, so filing one takes it OUT of the tree that emits it. A resource that is in no repository is a no-op with a note."
                 :examples ["repository unplace rechnung-view-full"
                            "repository unplace parse-konten derive-konten"]
                 :see-also [:repo-place :repositories :clone]}
  :repositories {:theme :code
                 :purpose "List the Source Repositories on the branch — each with its owning project, type, and how many transforms/functions it holds. CRUD goes through the schema-mutation chokepoint like every entity."
                 :examples ["repository list" "repositories" "repositories --json"]
                 :see-also [:new-repository :projects :clone]}
  :project-files {:theme :project
                  :purpose "Read the kompass FILESYSTEM tree — folders + resource placements. Placement labels resolve through the live schema, and each placement carries its ANNOTATIONS: ★ curated, [trash] and #tags. Those lived in the browser's localStorage, so this pane could not show them at all — a resource somebody had trashed listed here as an ordinary file."
                  :examples ["project files"]
                  :see-also [:place :projects :project-map]}
  :place {:theme :project
          :purpose "File resource(s) into the kompass tree. Each resource name resolves to its RID handle via the live schema; --into names a target folder (found, or created at the root if new); no --into lands it at the root. Idempotent — re-placing a resource reparents it. --as overrides a single placement's display name."
          :examples ["project place bestellung"
                     "project place bestellung lieferant --into ersatzteile"
                     "project place raw_messages --as inbox"]
          :see-also [:project-files :projects :new-project :unplace]}
  :unplace {:theme :project
            :purpose "Remove resource(s) from the kompass tree — the inverse of `place`. Deletes the PLACEMENT node (a resource is filed at most once); the resource itself (its object-type/transform/dataset/…) is untouched. Resources are addressed by name, resolved the same way `place` resolves them; a resource that isn't filed is a no-op with a note."
            :examples ["project unplace bestellung" "project unplace bestellung lieferant"]
            :see-also [:place :project-files :folder]}
  :folder {:theme :project
           :purpose "Author the kompass FOLDER tree: `project folder create <name> [--into <parent>]`, `rename <folder> <new-name>`, `move <folder> [--into <parent>]`, `delete <folder>`. Folders are addressed by name against the live tree; --into names a parent (found-or-created at root; absent ⇒ root). Delete reparents any contents up — placements are never orphaned."
           :examples ["project folder create ersatzteile"
                      "project folder create reports --into ersatzteile"
                      "project folder rename reports berichte"
                      "project folder move berichte --into ersatzteile"
                      "project folder delete berichte"]
           :see-also [:place :project-files :projects]}
  :llm-usage  {:theme :llm
               :purpose "Where LLMs are used across BOTH planes. Scans the schema: BATCH (a :language-model input on a transform or query-function), INTERACTIVE (an agent :model), and EMBEDDING (object :semantic-search :model) — one PLANE/WHERE/MODEL table. The read that answers 'is bulk LLM work in the batch plane where it belongs?'."
               :see-also [:agent-list :audit :llm :llm-next]}
  :llm-next   {:theme :llm
               :purpose "WHEN model spend happens next. Every build schedule whose resolved build SCOPE contains a :language-model transform, with its next fire time — plus the paused ones that would start spending the moment they are unpaused. The scope is resolved server-side by the same code the build runs, so a schedule that reaches a Grok transform only via :scope :downstream (an import target that looks unrelated) still shows up."
               :examples ["llm next"]
               :patterns
               ["Run it after topping up a provider balance, and before unpausing an ingest schedule — it answers 'what will this cost me and when' without starting anything."]
               :see-also [:llm-usage :schedule-list :build-estimate :llm]}
  :schedules   {:theme :operate
                :purpose "Declare / upsert build schedules (time-triggered target builds)."
                :patterns
                ["Give each schedule a descriptive name and enable retries + abort-on-failure; keep one schedule per pipeline."]
                :anti-patterns [{:rule "Don't schedule the same target dataset from two schedules."
                                 :why "Two schedules on one dataset build it twice and wait on each other."}]
                :see-also [:schedule :run-import]}
  :health-checks {:theme :operate
                  :purpose "Declare / upsert Data-Health checks (freshness / row-count / null-% / uniqueness / custom over a node's table)."
                  :patterns
                  ["Bind each check to an object-type :target and pick a :severity (:moderate | :critical); a :freshness check needs the timestamp :column, a :row-count a :min, a :custom its own read-only SELECT."]
                  :anti-patterns
                  [{:rule "Don't reach for a :custom SQL check when a built-in :kind (freshness/row-count/null-percentage/approximate-unique) expresses it."
                    :why "The built-ins resolve the node's physical table + column for you and stay correct across renames; a hand-written :query hard-codes the physical spelling."}]
                  :see-also [:schedules :run-import]}
  :schedule    {:theme :operate
                :purpose "Control one build schedule: status / run / pause / unpause / delete."
                :patterns
                ["If a schedule has failed repeatedly, pause it and fix the failing target rather than leaving it retrying on every tick."]
                :anti-patterns
                [{:rule "Frozen ingest? Check the PAUSE state FIRST — before any watermark or incremental theory."
                  :why "`schedule list` shows every schedule's target, cadence and paused flag. A paused critical schedule is SILENT behind green health checks — there is no freshness alarm — so the mundane cause hides behind the sophisticated lore (stuck cursors, incremental skips, reindex hot-loops) that fills the rest of this registry. The mailbox ingest once sat frozen 12 days on a hand-paused `imap-pull` while a long detour went through source-reachability and stuck-cursor hypotheses. Also don't read a neighbouring schedule's green `runs` as proof yours is alive."}
                 {:rule "Don't assume `schedule <name> --do run` re-pulls the external system."
                  :why "It runs a target-scoped build; if the schedule's :target is a DOWNSTREAM object-type (not the pull's landing dataset) the external pull never executes — you get a ~1 s run with an empty import. Verify with `run <id>` (real pull = non-trivial pull phase + rows on the pull transform). To force the pull use a full `run-import` (or `run-import --only <pull_transform>`)."}]
                :examples ["schedule imap-pull --do status" "schedule imap-pull --do run"]
                :see-also [:run-import :run :incremental-sync]}
  :watch       {:theme :operate
                :purpose "Follow a live build, 1s: build/phase/transform boundaries AND every model call — the prompt going out and the answer (or the provider's error) coming back. Streams an in-process event ring, so it keeps answering while the build holds the control plane."
                :when "Watching a build actually work — which mail is being extracted right now, what the model replied, which call failed and WHY. This is the verb for 'what is it doing?'; `jobs <run-id>` answers 'where does it stand?' after the fact, and `run <run-id>` answers 'why did it fail?'."
                :patterns
                ["`--jobs` adds the per-transform roster, and is opt-in for a reason: the roster is a control-plane read and a build holds the control plane (measured: eight of ten roster polls returned nothing after 35s mid-build). The event ring cannot be blocked that way, so the stream that always works is the default."
                 "The window is a bounded in-process ring — it starts with the current process and a redeploy empties it. The durable records are `llm audit` (per call, verbatim request/response) and `jobs` (per transform); `watch` is the window, not the archive."
                 "Every event carries a monotonic seq. `--once` prints the cursor and `--since <seq>` resumes there."
                 "`--llm` narrows to model calls, `--build` to build boundaries and job log lines, `--errors` to WARN+ERROR, `--job <transform>` to one job's lines, `--full` leaves prompt/answer untrimmed."
                 "A running python job's stderr streams in as `│ <transform> <line>`, coloured by level."]
                :examples ["watch" "watch --llm" "watch --errors" "watch --jobs"
                           "watch --once --json" "watch --since 412"]
                :see-also [:jobs :run :llm :trace]}
  :stop        {:theme :operate
                :purpose "Cancel a running build by --id or --all (cooperative — aborts at the next transform)."}

  :branch      {:theme :version
                :purpose "Fork a child branch off a parent (default main) and check it out."
                :see-also [:checkout]}
  :checkout    {:theme :version
                :purpose "Switch the session to an existing branch (this client only; the server keeps no branch)."
                :see-also [:branch]}
  :branches    {:theme :version
                :purpose "Every branch with its GOVERNANCE state: protected?, approvals required, tip-commit age, last committer, and its lifecycle status when not Active (inactive · closed · merged, with \"data deleted\" once purged). `--closed` includes Closed and Merged branches (hidden by default)."
                :when "Before proposing or committing: it tells you whether your target takes direct changes at all, and whose branches are already in flight."
                :patterns
                ["Branch names carry provenance — `pipeline/<seed>/<label>` from `branch-pipeline`, `staging/<user>/<parent>` for a staged batch, `run/<parent>/<id>` for a live build."]
                :see-also [:protect :propose :close]}
  :ask-review  {:theme :version
                :purpose "`ask-review <id> [--reviewer a,b]`. Its own act rather than a side effect of pushing: a branch is SAVED continuously, and reviewers hear about a proposal only once it is put into review."
                :when "When the work on the branch is ready to be looked at — not when it is merely pushed. Before this, the branch is a working state; after it, it is a request for someone's attention."
                :patterns
                ["The reviewer list is a courtesy, not a gate: the TARGET's `review` rules (`protect`) decide, per resource, who must approve — by default only an owner of a resource someone else BREAKS."
                 "Zustimmung fällt danach pro Ressource — `proposal show <id>` listet die Tasks, `approve <id> --task <task>` beurteilt einen einzelnen."]
                :anti-patterns [{:rule "Don't ask for review to make a push count."
                                 :why "The push already ran the gates and the checks already say what they say. This changes who is expected to look, and asking before the work is ready trains reviewers to ignore the request."}]
                :examples ["ask-review 14b66dc3-e964-4be8-9c6f-7058c6e325b6"
                           "ask-review 14b66dc3 --reviewer alma,ben"]
                :see-also [:propose :approve :proposal :merge-proposal]}
  :protect     {:theme :version
                :purpose "WITHOUT A FLAG IT READS the branch's effective policy and writes nothing; `--on` is what protects. A protected branch refuses direct schema changes, staged commits AND direct git pushes — changes must arrive as a PROPOSAL from another branch. `--require-checks` adds the other half of the rule: the MERGE refuses a proposal whose checks are not green (`--no-require-checks` turns it off). `--review <edn>` sets the review rules — a vector of `{:when [predicate …] :by [approver …] :count n}`; predicates :always :breaking :accretion :foreign [:section s] [:project p], approvers :owner :other [:principal name]; `--default-review` returns to the default `[{:when [:breaking :foreign] :by [:owner]}]`. `--auto-land` lands a proposal on proposing when no rule requires a verdict — it never approves in the author's name (`--no-auto-land` turns it off). `<branch>` may be `*`: the default every branch inherits. Any policy field the server has can also be set as `field=value` (e.g. `merge-modes=squash,merge`), typed as the server declares it. Only the flags you pass are written, so setting one never resets another."
                :when "The branch backs something live and you want changes reviewed rather than merely isolated."
                :patterns
                ["Protecting the TRUNK is what makes 'the commit is in git and NOT in the ontology' unreachable there: the push is refused, the refs do not move, and the author works on a branch instead (`sutra branch <name>`)."
                 "`protect <branch>` bare is the READ — that is how you check a policy before changing it."]
                :anti-patterns [{:rule "Don't protect a branch to make it private."
                                 :why "Protection restrains WRITES, not reads. Every builder can see and check out every branch; branch listings are global by design."}
                                {:rule "Don't set `--review '[]'` to unblock yourself."
                                 :why "It removes every review rule on that branch, also the one that makes an owner judge a breaking change to their resource. If a task waits, it waits for the person whose resource you break — ask them."}
                                {:rule "Don't protect the trunk without --require-checks."
                                 :why "Protection alone only closes the front door: a proposal with red checks can still be merged, which is the same unchecked change taking the long way round."}
                                {:rule "Don't set `*` unless you mean EVERY branch."
                                 :why "`*` is the enrollment default and is inherited field-by-field by every branch that has no row of its own — including branches that do not exist yet. `protect * --on` makes every new branch refuse a direct push, and the refusal names that one branch, which reads as 'this branch is special' rather than 'all of them are'."}]
                :examples ["protect main" "protect main --on --require-checks"
                           "protect main --review '[{:when [:always] :by [:other]}]'"
                           "protect main --default-review"
                           "protect * --retention-days 30 --inactive-days 7" "protect main --off"]
                :see-also [:propose :approve :branch-roles :branches]}
  :branch-roles {:theme :version
                 :purpose "Who OWNS a branch, and may therefore land its proposals: `roles <branch>` lists, `--own <user>` grants, `--revoke <user>` removes. A branch with NO owner is ungoverned — anyone may land its work — and says so."
                 :when "Before relying on the owner check, and whenever a branch's author hands work over."
                 :patterns
                 ["Ownership is recorded automatically for a branch created via `branch <name>`; branches predating the roles table have none, and stay open until someone claims them."]
                 :anti-patterns [{:rule "Don't revoke the last owner to 'open a branch up'."
                                  :why "It is refused. Assign the new owner first."}]
                 :examples ["roles feature/x" "roles feature/x --own alice"]
                 :see-also [:protect :merge-proposal]}
  :close       {:theme :version
                :purpose "CLOSE a branch: `close <branch>`. it cannot be reopened. Seven days later its data is deleted; its name, status and proposal history stay. A merged proposal's source branch becomes Merged on its own and needs no close."
                :when "A branch was abandoned and nothing on it will ever be proposed."
                :anti-patterns [{:rule "Don't close a branch you might pick up again."
                                 :why "There is no reopening; the work would have to be redone on a new branch. To mark it stale without ending it, leave it to the Inactive label (`sweep`), which `reactivate` takes off."}]
                :examples ["close feature/x"]
                :see-also [:reactivate :sweep :branches :drop-branch]}
  :reactivate  {:theme :version
                :purpose "Take the INACTIVE label off a branch: `reactivate <branch>` (branch owner only). Inactive is a reversible label; a Closed or Merged branch stays as it is."
                :examples ["reactivate feature/x"]
                :see-also [:close :sweep :branches]}
  :remove-resources {:theme :version
                     :purpose "Take resources off a branch: `branch remove <branch> <section/rid…>`. Each returns to what the branch's target holds; a removal that another change on the branch depends on is refused."
                     :when "A resource on the branch should not be part of its proposal."
                     :examples ["branch remove feature/x object-types/ri.sutra.main.object-type.abc"]
                     :see-also [:branches :propose]}
  :drop-branch {:theme :version
                :purpose "CLOSE a branch AND delete its data now: `branch delete <name>`. The same terminal Closed as `close`, without the seven-day grace; name, status and proposal history stay. Prompts before acting. Without a terminal (a script, a piped batch) the prompt DECLINES and nothing happens — `--yes` answers it."
                :when "A branch is finished and its data must go now rather than after the seven-day grace."
                :anti-patterns [{:rule "Don't delete when `close` would do."
                                 :why "Both are terminal, but `close` keeps the data seven days — time to notice a mistake and pull something off the branch before it is gone."}]
                :examples ["branch delete feature/x" "branch delete feature/x --yes"]
                :see-also [:close :branches :sweep]}

  :preview-staged {:theme :version
                   :purpose "The COMMIT WINDOW: `changeset preview` — what `changeset commit` would actually do. Answers :clean | :conflicts | :no-base, lists the staged changes, and names each conflicting entity."
                   :when "Before any commit that matters, and always when someone else has been working on the same branch's parent."
                   :patterns
                   ["The two storage tiers answer at different GRAIN and both are honest: the control tier reports per ontological entity (`object-types/widget`), the store reports per row — where the whole schema is one row, so any concurrent change collides on `schema_definition`."
                    "`:no-base` is neither clean nor conflicting: the branch forked before a base was recorded, so the check cannot tell. The commit path refuses on it."]
                   :examples ["changeset preview" "changeset preview --json"]
                   :see-also [:rebase-staged :commit :review :check]}
  :rebase-staged  {:theme :version
                   :purpose "Settle the staged batch's conflicts ON THE BRANCH: `changeset rebase [--mine <entity>…] [--theirs <entity>…]`. Entities are named exactly as `changeset preview` prints them (`<class>/<key>`); anything you don't name defaults to --theirs."
                   :when "`changeset preview` came back :conflicts and you want the following commit to be a clean atomic apply."
                   :patterns
                   ["after a rebase the branch's only divergence is what it changed since, so the commit has nothing left to merge."]
                   :anti-patterns [{:rule "Don't blanket `--mine` to make conflicts go away."
                                    :why "Every entity you claim overwrites whatever the parent landed there — the exact last-writer-wins outcome a merge exists to prevent. Name only the entities you actually own."}]
                   :examples ["changeset rebase" "changeset rebase --mine object-types/widget"]
                   :see-also [:preview-staged :commit]}
  :discard-one    {:theme :version
                   :purpose "Drop ONE staged action by its seq: `changeset drop <seq>`. The batch-wide form is `changeset discard`."
                   :when "The staged batch is right except for one entry — surgical removal beats discarding and re-authoring the lot."
                   :see-also [:review :preview-staged]}

  :rollback    {:theme :version
                :purpose "Roll a node's table back to an earlier version: `dataset rollback <name> --to <ref> [--kind dataset|object-type|transform]`. Lands as a new :rollback Run, so the rollback is itself in the history rather than a silent rewrite."
                :when "A build wrote wrong rows and you want the previous contents back without re-deriving them."
                :patterns
                ["`--to` takes a VERSION TOKEN whose SHAPE picks the substrate — a snapshot id. The server owns that resolution, so the same verb keeps working across the lake cutover."
                 "Read `dataset history <name>` first: it prints the typed transaction chain with the commit per transaction, which is where the token comes from."]
                :anti-patterns [{:rule "Rolling back DATA does not roll back LOGIC."
                                 :why "The transform that produced the bad rows is unchanged and the next build will reproduce them. Fix the logic (and bump `--semantic-version`) as well."}]
                :examples ["dataset history rechnungen"
                           "dataset rollback rechnungen --to abc123def0"]
                :see-also [:transactions :consumption :delta :force-snapshot]}

  :version-show {:theme :version
                 :purpose "Read ONE resource's definition as of an earlier version: `version show <object-type|transform|source> <name> --as-of <ref>`."
                 :when "Answering 'what did this look like before?' — a diff by eye against the live `describe`, without moving the session or checking anything out."
                 :patterns
                 ["Read-only and branch-neutral: nothing is checked out and the session's branch does not move. Sutra-OS keeps no parallel saved-version list — the commit graph already holds every version of every resource, so this is an ADDRESSING surface over it."]
                 :examples ["version show transform rechnungs-parse --as-of abc123def0"]
                 :see-also [:version-rows :transactions :restore]}
  :version-rows {:theme :version
                 :purpose "Read an object type's ROWS as of an earlier version: `version rows <type> --as-of <ref> [--limit N]` — the historical dataset view."
                 :when "Confirming what a rollback would restore, or what a build changed, before acting."
                 :examples ["version rows rechnung --as-of abc123def0 --limit 20"]
                 :see-also [:version-show :rollback :transactions]}
  :sweep       {:theme :version
                :purpose "Apply the RETENTION policy: label a branch inactive once its tip commit is older than `--inactive-days`, release the label if it moved again, CLOSE it (terminal) past `--days N` / its own `--retention-days`, and delete the data of branches Closed or Merged more than seven days ago. It also CLOSES open proposals whose source branch is gone: there is nothing left to merge from, so Closed (not merged) is the only end they have. PREVIEWS by default — `--apply` to act."
                :when "Branches are meant to be short-lived; a stagnant branch keeps a whole copy of the ontology alive."
                :patterns
                ["A server with `settings retention-ms` set runs exactly this every N ms, so the manual verb is for previewing the plan and for one-off cleanups."]
                :anti-patterns
                [{:rule "Retention is opt-in — a bare `sweep` with no --days and no configured retention does nothing."
                  :why "Silently closing other people's branches — for good — on a default policy would be worse than doing nothing."}]
                :examples ["sweep --days 30" "sweep --days 30 --apply"]
                :see-also [:close :reactivate :protect]}
  :propose     {:theme :version
                :purpose "PROPOSE a change: `propose [title] [--target <branch>] [--source <branch>] [--desc …]`. Opens a reviewable request to merge one branch into another (default target `main`, default source your current branch) and reports the conflict pre-check."
                :when "The target branch is protected, or you simply want the change reviewed before it lands."
                :patterns
                ["THESE VERBS ARE THE OPERATOR HALF, and they address a proposal BY ID. The author half: `branch <name>` → `--branch <name> projection push` → `--branch <name> commit` → `propose --source <name> --target main` → these verbs."
                 "A git push of Python onto a branch with no open proposal is refused in `pre-receive` (`no open changeset — NOTHING was written`): `branch <name>` → `propose --source <name>` FIRST, then `git push`."
                 "The operator loop remains right for what it is: proposing across branches you are not standing in, and for instance work with no checkout — `propose --target main --source <branch>` → `approve <id>` → `proposal merge <id>`."]
                :see-also [:approve :merge-proposal :proposals :protect]}
  :proposal    {:theme :version
                :purpose "One proposal in full: `proposal <id>` — title, description, source→target, author, who has approved it so far, and any REJECTION (which blocks the merge until withdrawn)."
                :see-also [:proposals :approve :reject :merge-proposal]}
  :proposals   {:theme :version
                :purpose "List proposals: open by default, `--status all|merged|closed`, `--target <branch>` and `--source <branch>` to narrow. Shows source→target, status and the approval count against the bar."
                :patterns
                ["Narrow it. The listing judges each row — its stage, its open tasks, its checks — so an unfiltered list of every open proposal is the slowest read in this group; `--source <branch>` answers in one round trip."]
                :see-also [:propose :approve]}
  :approve     {:theme :version
                :purpose "Approve a proposal by id, optionally `--note <why>`. Which tasks you may judge and whether your verdict counts follows the target's `review` rules per resource (default: an owner of a resource the change breaks; an addition waits for no one). Verdicts are appended — a later one supersedes yours, the earlier stays recorded — and they count with your rights as they stand when the proposal lands."
                :see-also [:propose :reject :merge-proposal]
                :patterns ["Approve only where `proposal <id>` lists a task that requires it."]}
  :reject      {:theme :version
                :purpose "Reject a proposal (`reject <id> [--note why]`). The proposal stays OPEN — the author pushes a fix to the source branch and the same reviewer approves, which overwrites the rejection."
                :when "The change is wrong, but the request itself is still live. Use `close-proposal` when the request should go away entirely."
                :anti-patterns [{:rule "Don't use `close-proposal` as a rejection."
                                 :why "Closing ends the request; rejecting blocks the merge while leaving the author a way to fix it in place."}]
                :see-also [:approve :close-proposal :merge-proposal]}
  :merge-proposal {:theme :version
                   :purpose "Land an approved proposal (`proposal merge <id>`): merges its source branch into its target once its BRANCH OWNER asks for it, no reviewer has rejected it, the approval bar is met and the merge is conflict-free. Records the merge commit on the proposal."
                   :anti-patterns [{:rule "Don't reach for `merge-proposal` to escape protection."
                                    :why "It refuses while a task the target's `review` rules require lacks a fresh approval from someone the rule accepts (rights read NOW), on any rejection, on conflicts, when the proposal cannot be judged (tasks unreadable), when the source or target moved since it was judged, and for anyone who is not an owner of the source branch (or the admin)."}]
                   :patterns
                   ["Read `proposal <id>` first: it names what is still required. Never chain this after a push that may have failed — an empty merge closes the branch."
                    "`empty` is the only preview status that means there is nothing to land. `no-base` and `unreadable` mean the preview could not be computed — the branch may well carry work, and merging on either reads as 'nothing to do' while closing it."]
                   :see-also [:propose :approve :reject :branch-roles]}
  :close-proposal {:theme :version :purpose "Withdraw or reject a proposal without merging it."
                   :see-also [:propose]}
  :login       {:theme :version :purpose "Authenticate from the local creds store."}}

 :concepts
 {:primary-key
  {:purpose "The deterministic identifier of an object type — what makes two rows the SAME object across builds."
   :patterns
   ["Derive the key from stable business fields (an order number, an email message-id), so the same real thing keeps the same key every build."
    "User edits are stored against the primary-key value, so changing an object type's primary key discards every existing edit."]
   :anti-patterns
   [{:rule
     "Primary keys must be deterministic — never mint one from a row number or a random value."
     :why "A key that differs on the next build makes a new object; the edits and links on the old one are orphaned."}
    {:rule "Make sure the key is unique per row before assigning it."
     :why "Two rows with the same primary key fail the Conductor build."}]}
  :object-type
  {:purpose "A class of objects in the ontology (a noun: Order, Email, Location) backed by a dataset, with typed properties, a primary key, and a title."
   :patterns
   ["Give it a clear human name + title property; back it with a datasource + deterministic key mapping."
    "Every object type needs at least one property, a primary key, and a title key (the display name shown across apps)."]
   :anti-patterns [{:rule "Avoid cryptic incrementing names (type1, type2, dataset1)."
                    :why "They make the ontology unreadable and hard to refactor."}
                   {:rule "Don't back two object types with the same datasource."
                    :why "Each datasource backs exactly one object type; reusing one fails on save (DatasetAndBranchAlreadyRegistered)."}]
   :see-also [:primary-key :link-type :interface]}
  :link-type
  {:purpose "A typed relationship between two object types (from → to) with a cardinality — the edges of the ontology graph."
   :patterns
   ["Name the relationship from the perspective that reads naturally; set cardinality to match reality (one-to-many vs many-to-many)."
    "A foreign-key link matches one object's FK property to the other's primary key; only a many-to-many link needs a backing join-table dataset — and that backing is required to write back / edit the link."]
   :anti-patterns [{:rule "Don't rely on cardinality to enforce uniqueness."
                    :why "One-to-one / many-to-one cardinality is an intent indicator only — the platform does not enforce it."}
                   {:rule "Don't back a non-many-to-many link with a materialised edge-pair JOIN TABLE or a :resolve-by-shared-property (non-PK match) — the FK-backing commit gate rejects both fail-closed. `describe <link>` shows the current `backing`."
                    :why "every other cardinality resolves the target on its PRIMARY KEY, i.e. a foreign key on the many side. Convert with {:fk …} (raw FK already on the many side) or {:fk-resolve …} (compute a non-PK/fuzzy match into an FK upstream)."}]
   :see-also [:action :object-type :fk-resolve]}
  :fk-resolve
  {:purpose "Declared on a link tuple as `{:fk-resolve {:key <FK-side PK> :as <computed FK attr> :query \"<SQL joining the two endpoint TYPE NAMES>\"}}`."
   :patterns
   ["The `:query` references each endpoint by its TYPE NAME as a table and emits exactly (<key>, <as>) — the FK-side's own PK plus the resolved target PK. Fuzzy/compound/redirect logic goes in this join body; the primary producer is left untouched (the supplement backfills the FK by PK)."
    "`:as` becomes a REAL materialised column on the many side — queryable after the build, and the thing `{:fk}` resolves on."]
   :anti-patterns
   [{:rule "`:as` auto-declares as an attribute ONLY when the FK-side object-type is present in the SAME .ent — a link-only .ent commits, then the gate rejects it (':as is not a declared attribute on <type>')."
     :why "The sugar appends `:as` to the object-type's attribute list in the file; with no :objects entry there is nothing to append to. Include the FK-side object-type in the .ent WITH ITS FULL attribute list (a targeted object-type decl REPLACES the attr list — an omitted attr, incl the PK, is DROPPED), and the sugar adds the FK column alongside."}]
   :see-also [:link-type :primary-key :object-type]}
  :interface
  {:purpose "An abstract type a set of object types implement — lets you query/act across implementers polymorphically."
   :patterns
   ["Model shared capability as an interface; endpoints resolve across ALL implementers (a UNION), not a single table."
    "Prefer properties declared on the interface itself to shared properties; one object type may implement as many interfaces as it has roles."
    "EDIT across implementers with the interface action rules — `{:op :create-of-interface :interface I :object-type T :values …}` / `:update-of-interface` / `:delete-of-interface` in an action's :rules. One action for every implementer; it may write only properties the INTERFACE declares (never a type-specific one, never a primary key), and a create names the concrete type — usually from a parameter."]
   :anti-patterns [{:rule "Don't assume an interface has its own instance table."
                    :why "Interfaces are abstract — not backed by datasets and never instantiated directly; a read unions the concrete implementers."}]
   :see-also [:object-type :shared-property]}
  :shared-property
  {:purpose
   "A property definition reused across multiple object types (consistent name/type/semantics)."
   :patterns
   ["Reach for a shared property when the same property (start date, status) appears on several object types — its definition lives in one place, while each object type keeps its own values."]
   :see-also [:object-type]}
  :derived-property
  {:purpose "A value CALCULATED AT RUNTIME from an object's own fields OR its linked objects' (unit price, a line total, Σ of the linked invoices, a linked order's status) — a :derived property, not a transform edit. Nothing is stored, so it can never drift from its source of truth: the arithmetic kind is evaluated from the row every read, and a `:rollup` traverses the link live on the same read."
   :patterns
   ["Prefer a read-time :derived property over baking the computation into a transform or hardcoding it."
    "Derived values are computed at request time from the object's own properties OR its linked objects', and can then be filtered/sorted/aggregated within that SAME request — so 'I need this type to show a related object's field' is a derived property across the link, not a new stored column."
    "A `:rollup` (Σ/count/latest-by over a link) is read-time too, so a link an Action just wrote shows up in the next read — no build in between."
    "There is NO way to store a derived property, and none is needed: a pipeline reads stored properties and joins linked data itself, an app or Function asks for the derived value at request time. A value that must live in a column of the OBJECT TYPE is dataset-layer work — compute it in a transform, and it is an ordinary property, not a :derived one."]
   :anti-patterns
   [{:rule "Don't copy a linked object's property into a stored column on this type (a transform that joins the neighbour and emits its field as your own column)."
     :why "The copy is a snapshot that silently goes stale when the source object changes, and it duplicates one fact in two places. Select it live across the link instead: a :derived property (read time) or a `query <type> --where <link>.<attr>=…` filter. Store only the FOREIGN KEY (the link itself), never the neighbour's non-key fields."}
    {:rule "If you search or filter on a value, store it — a derived one can't serve that."
     :why "A derived property exists only when read, so the search index never sees it — full-text search and keyword filters can't reach it."}
    {:rule "Don't reach for a stored column to make a derived value visible to a transform."
     :why "A transform input and a materialization hold stored properties only: join the linked data in the transform. What belongs in a column of the object type itself is dataset-layer work: compute it in a transform, and it is an ordinary property with no :derived at all."}]
   :see-also [:transform :query :link-type :materialization]}
  :materialization
  {:purpose "A DATASET built from the ONTOLOGY — every object's current state, source rows with user edits applied, written out as a table. The only thing in the schema that reads the ontology, and what a pipeline reads when it needs ontology state."
   :when "A transform needs an object type's rows; a download/export needs the current ontology as a table."
   :patterns
   ["Declare it on the dataset, not the transform: `:datasets {:zeitbuchung_mat {:kind :materialization :object-type :zeitbuchung}}`. Install lifts it into a `<name>-materialize` transform (the mirror of `<name>-sync`) — one more build node, scheduled after the type's producers."
    "NO `:columns` — the columns follow the object type's properties, derived ones included. `:properties [...]` narrows it to a subset; omit for all."
    "It reflects the last COMPLETED publish of the type, not rows staged mid-build: a consumer sees this build's objects in the NEXT build. That is the honest reading — mid-build the links are not published yet, and a rollup derived then would answer about a half-built graph."
    "Row identity is the OBJECT's id, so re-materializing updates rows in place and the downstream change feed stays O(changed)."]
   :anti-patterns
   [{:rule "Create a materialization only when it is needed: a pipeline that needs the state WITH user edits, or a download. Otherwise a pipeline reads the object type directly."
     :why "`lint` names a materialization over a type no action edits `:materialization-without-edits`. It lags one build, the producer's output does not: its reader builds after it and sees it in the same build. The object table (`:object-backing`) is no substitute, it has no build edge (`:transform-reads-object-table`)."}
    {:rule "Don't give a materialization an `:external-source` or authored `:columns`."
     :why "Both make it a second, disagreeing producer of one table. The grammar refuses the combination."}]
   :examples
   [":datasets {:zeitbuchung_mat {:kind :materialization :object-type :zeitbuchung :properties [:datum :stunden :summe-kosten]}}"]
   :see-also [:transform :derived-property :dataset-preview]}
  :application
  {:purpose "An App Studio app the end user gets — an :application object in the schema. A DATA app carries its view code as cljs `:source` (SCI-interpreted in the browser, no rebuild); a declarative app carries only a `:layout`."
   :when "To give a non-technical user (a Buchhalter, a Sachbearbeiter) a point-and-click surface over one object type — a worklist + a form/action — instead of the raw ontology explorer."
   :patterns
   ["Author it like a python transform: an .ent with `:schema {:applications {<name> {:object-type <type> :project <proj> :status :published :icon \"…\" :description \"…\" :source-file \"apps/<name>.cljs\"}}}` plus the sibling `apps/<name>.cljs`, both in the project's code repository, shipped with `projection push` (code-repository) — the publish check inlines `:source-file` to `:source`. Installing the .ent from disk is retired."
    "Round-trip an EXISTING data-app with `clone <project> <dir>` → edit `apps/<name>.cljs` → `push <dir>`. (Only apps that carry a `:source` are checked out; a built-in compiled app has none, so clone reports 'no app views'.)"
    "Back every WRITE the app makes with an Action Type, and put the business rule in the Action's submission criteria — the app just invokes it. The app stays a thin view; the rule can't be bypassed."
    "`apps` lists what's installed; the shipped consumer app (procurement-tracking) is the reference shape."]
   :anti-patterns
   [{:rule "Don't hardcode an app-specific backend route; user apps are data + a generic runtime."
     :why "The app is an :application object rendered by a pluggable runtime — a bespoke server endpoint defeats the point and won't deploy via install/push."}]
   :examples ["apps" "clone ersatzteile-portal /tmp/ep   # then edit apps/*.cljs, push"]
   :see-also [:action :automate :object-type]}
  :transform
  {:purpose "Code (SQL / Python / Clojure) that builds a DATASET — the pipeline layer beneath the ontology. The chain is `source → dataset → transform → CLEAN DATASET → object type`: pipeline work (parsing, joining, dedup, aggregation, FK resolution) happens BETWEEN datasets, and an object type is what the final curated dataset is shaped into. `:language` picks the executor: :sql runs in the compute tier, :python in a bubblewrap subprocess, :clojure in-process under an interpreter sandbox with no host access."
   :patterns
   ["THE LAYER RULE — a transform outputs a `:dataset` unless it is the LAST hop, the one shaping a curated dataset into an object type. Two transforms, not one: `parse → :dataset` then `shape → :object-type`. Splitting also pays for itself operationally — the expensive extract becomes a checkpoint you can build and eyeball alone (`run-import --only <dataset>`), and the cheap shaping re-runs for free with no re-Grok. A datasource SYNC lands in a dataset the same way: put `:external-source` on a `:datasets` entry (→ a `<name>-sync` transform), NOT on an object type."
    "Bias to smaller transforms chained together with snake_case column names; explicitly cast/validate types at the raw→clean boundary so a source schema change fails loudly."
    "Pick the language by what the work needs, not by taste: :pg-sql, :sqlite-sql or :mssql-sql for set operations over tables (it never leaves the engine); :python when you need the wheelhouse (pandas, pdfminer) or an EXTERNAL transform with egress; :clojure for row-shaping/derivation logic you want to run with no subprocess and no JSON round-trip (values keep their types — a decimal stays a decimal)."
    "A :clojure body evaluates to `(fn [{:keys [<input alias> …]}] …)` returning row maps — or a map of output-name → rows for a multi-output transform. The runtime surface mirrors the python lib: `sutra/previous-output`, `sutra/output-enum`, `sutra/cap-batch`, `sutra/trace`, `sutra/put-media-item`, `sutra/media-bytes`, `sutra/chat`."
    "A :clojure body can carry its OWN wiring, like python's @transform decorator — then the .ent block is just `{:language :clojure :code-file \"transforms/foo.clj\"}` and the file round-trips through `clone`/`push` alone. Two spellings, same spec map: `(sutra/transform {:inputs … :output … :primary-key … :incremental true} (fn [inputs] …))`, or the defn attr-map `(defn foo {:sutra/transform {…}} [inputs] …)` as the LAST form. Declaring is optional and never beats an explicit .ent value (install merges inferred-under-authored); a misspelled spec field is refused at save, not ignored."
    "A :clojure transform is netless BY CONSTRUCTION — no interop means no sockets, so `:external-systems` is refused rather than ignored. The one way out is a :language-model input via `sutra/chat`, which routes through the audited Language Model Service. Anything needing real egress is a :python transform."
    "DATA EXPECTATIONS (`Check(E…)`): a transform's invariant sits on its input or output — `:output {… :checks [{:name \"one invoice, one row\" :expectation [:group-by [:invoice_id] :is-unique] :on-error :fail}]}`, a pre-condition under `:inputs {:x {… :checks […]}}`; in Python `Output(…, checks=[Check(E.group_by('invoice_id').is_unique(), '…', on_error='FAIL')])`. The expression is the E chain as EDN (`[:col :a [:gt 0]]`, `[:col :t :max-value [:lt …]]`, `[:primary-key :a]`, `[:count [:equals [:dataset-ref :x :count]]]`, `[:schema [:contains {…}]]`, `:all/:any/:negate/:when`). It runs IN THE BUILD on the whole dataset: `:fail` (default) aborts the job and leaves its outputs as they were; `:warn` only reports. Every result lands in Data Health (kind `expectation`, on the transform) — the NAME is the identity; renamed, its history is gone. A health check (`:health-checks`) monitors from outside; an expectation guards."]
   :anti-patterns
   [{:rule "Make a field numeric only when you will calculate with it; an ID or a postcode stays a string."
     :why "Codes/ids that look numeric (postal codes, phone numbers) break when cast to numbers."}
    {:rule "A transform's output row KEYS must match derive-col (umlaut-fold ∘ kebab→snake) or the column lands nil."
     :why "Emitting 'anhänge' when the importer wants 'anhaenge' silently nulls the column. (A :clojure transform is the exception: the runner files each cell under BOTH the key as written and its derive-col spelling, so an idiomatic `:doc-number` also lands in `doc_number`.)"}
    {:rule "Don't write a transform that joins a LINKED object-type just to emit its property as a column on this type."
     :why "That denormalizes the neighbour's field into a stored copy that goes stale the moment the source object changes — the load-bearing rule is you never copy a linked object's property into a stored column, you select it live across the link. Model it as a :derived property (computed at read time from the linked object) or reach it with `query <type> --where <link>.<attr>=…`; the transform should store only the FOREIGN KEY that makes the link, not the neighbour's non-key fields."}
    {:rule "Don't chain OBJECT TYPES as pipeline intermediates: a transform that READS an object-type and WRITES another object-type is dataset-layer work hosted in the ontology."
     :why "The ontology is not the pipeline's scratch space. Aggregating a 76k-row object type into three more object types, or reading an object type to stamp a `resolved-*` FK column back onto itself, puts ETL in the layer end users read — they then see `resolved-bp-id`, `import-id`, `arbeitspaket-raw` next to business data. It also costs you reprocessing: `:semantic-version` auto-truncate fires only for `:dataset` outputs, so bumping it on an object-type producer re-runs the compute but does NOT clear the old rows. Terminate the step in a `:dataset` and back the object type off that; express a relationship as a LINK (`:endpoint-fk`), never as an FK column on the object."}
    {:rule "Don't bind `:external-source` to an OBJECT TYPE for a new sync — bind it to a `:datasets` entry."
     :why "An object-type binding lifts to a `<type>-rows` transform that lands raw ERP rows straight in the ontology with no curated layer, so every source column becomes a property and there is nowhere to cast, rename or drop ETL columns. A dataset binding lifts to `<name>-sync` and leaves you a shaping hop. The object-type form still works (existing syncs use it) but is not the shape to author new work against."}]
   :see-also [:source :run-import :derived-property :materialization]}
  :source
  {:purpose "An external system (REST/IMAP/DB) a transform pulls from, declared in the .ent and hosted as a network source over egress."
   :patterns
   ["Explicitly cast/validate the incoming columns at the raw→clean boundary so a change in the source schema surfaces as a build error instead of silently mis-typing a column."
    "An IMAP/REST source is pulled by an EXTERNAL TRANSFORM that lands a RAW dataset; the object-type is a downstream map of that dataset. So 'refresh the source' = run the external transform (full `run-import` / `--only <pull>`), not a build of the downstream object-type."]
   :see-also [:datasources :transform :incremental-sync]}
  :incremental-sync
  {:purpose "How an incremental pipeline decides what to FETCH from a source and what to RE-COMPUTE downstream — two independent mechanisms that are easy to conflate."
   :patterns
   ["Fetch scope = max(:since floor, cursor). An incremental external pull with a `:since` floor fetches from max(floor, high-water-mark), where the cursor is derived from the newest row ALREADY in the landing dataset. So once bootstrapped, the cursor dominates: LOWERING `:since` alone backfills nothing. To widen the window backward you must BOTH lower `:since` AND clear the landing dataset (an empty snapshot write) so the next pull re-bootstraps to the floor."
    "Re-compute scope is decoupled from fetch. A downstream incremental transform (LLM/derive) skips rows already in ITS OWN output — dedup by its output key via previous_output — not by the pull cursor. Clearing the raw landing dataset re-bootstraps the PULL but does NOT reset that downstream dedup, so a re-pull of the same rows costs no LLM: already-processed rows stay skipped by key."
    "Verify actual coverage from the data, not the config: `query <type> --show <date-col> --order <date-col>` (or a per-day tally) shows the real min/max the corpus holds — which can start later than `:since` if the pull was first bootstrapped after the Stichtag. `delta`/`transactions` show the per-transform consume/commit boundaries."
    "Two DIFFERENT backfills — don't conflate them: the FETCH-window backfill (older SOURCE rows) is widened by lowering `:since` + clearing the landing so the pull re-bootstraps; the SCHEMA backfill (a newly-added COLUMN onto already-landed rows) needs a `:semantic-version` bump on the producer. For a `:dataset` producer that `:semantic-version` bump ALSO fixes the `Unknown column '<col>'` APPEND crash a bare column-add causes — the merge-upsert can't write a column the frozen landing table lacks. See :reprocessing."]
   :anti-patterns [{:rule "Don't assume lowering the `:since` floor backfills older data."
                    :why "The fetch bound is max(:since, cursor); with a populated landing dataset the cursor wins and nothing older is fetched. Clear the landing dataset too, or the floor change is a no-op."}
                   {:rule "Don't assume re-pulling (or a landing-dataset clear) re-charges the LLM for already-processed rows."
                    :why "On a content-keyed (previous_output) incremental transform, dedup lives in the transform's own output keyed by the row id (e.g. message-id) — untouched by a landing-dataset clear — so already-processed rows are skipped. (A transaction-keyed dataframe('added') path would differ; check which is live before relying on this.)"}
                   {:rule "Don't commit a new output column on an incremental `:dataset` pull and rely on the schedule to pick it up."
                    :why "The scheduled incremental APPEND merges into the FROZEN landing schema and fails with `Unknown column '<col>'` on every tick — a code commit does NOT migrate the landing table. A column change needs a `:semantic-version` bump (one snapshot rewrite that widens the schema and backfills old rows), not just a push. See :reprocessing."}]
   :see-also [:source :transform :run-import :delta :transactions :reprocessing]}
  :reprocessing
  {:purpose "How to FORCE a transform to re-run after you change its LOGIC (not its inputs) — three levers people conflate, plus the link-edge gap that silently keeps stale rows."
   :patterns
   ["`:semantic-version` N→N+1 re-runs a PRODUCER's own logic: it resets the consumer pointer (⇒ full re-read) for EVERY output kind, but the explicit auto-TRUNCATE that clears the old rows fires only for a `:dataset` output. Which matters in exactly one case — an `:incremental` `:object-type`/`:link-type` producer. A NON-incremental one is already `snapshot-write?` (truncate + reconcile every build), so the bump changes nothing for it; an INCREMENTAL one keeps its `:modify` upsert write, so the compute re-runs and the stale rows stay."
    "That asymmetry is DELIBERATE, not a gap — do not 'fix' it by truncating object tables. An object-type's table also holds WRITEBACK rows (objects an Action created) and user edits. `reconcile-object-table!` sweeps source-deleted rows while EXEMPTING user create/update rows (see sutra.builds.ledger/record-edit-provenance!); a blind TRUNCATE has no such exemption and would destroy them — the same hazard as the `reindex` verb. So for an incremental object producer use `force-snapshot` on the producing dataset then re-run the producer, or make the write `:replace`. It is also a concrete reason to TERMINATE a transform in a `:dataset` and shape into the object type from there: the clean layer reprocesses cleanly, the ontology layer cannot."
    "A column ADD/RENAME on an `:incremental` `:dataset` producer is ITSELF a `:semantic-version` case (not just a code commit): the bump snapshot-rewrites the landing with the new schema AND backfills the column onto old rows. Without it the incremental APPEND crashes with `Unknown column` — see the anti-pattern."
    "`force-snapshot <dataset>` queues a snapshot on its PRODUCER: nothing changes until its next build, which reads every input in full and replaces its outputs; consumers then re-read the replaced dataset by themselves. The dataset and any object type on it stay readable until that build."
    "Confirm what ACTUALLY ran with `run <id>` / `jobs <run-id>`, and what's actually DEPLOYED by reading the live schema (`/api/schema` → :transforms → :query/:semantic-version). Don't infer execution from row counts — that's how an hour disappears."]
   :anti-patterns
   [{:rule "Don't add or rename a column on an `:incremental` `:dataset` producer without bumping `:semantic-version`."
     :why "The incremental APPEND merge-upserts by pk into the EXISTING landing table; a newly-emitted column is absent from that frozen physical schema, so the build fails with `Unknown column '<col>' in 'd_<uuid>'` — and keeps failing on EVERY scheduled tick until fixed (the pull dies before any job, so the run shows `jobs:[]`). Bump `:semantic-version` N→N+1 to force ONE non-incremental SNAPSHOT run: it rewrites the landing with the widened schema AND backfills the new column onto all prior rows (auto-truncate fires because the output is a `:dataset`)."}
    {:rule "Don't expect a link-type transform to DELETE the links you removed from its query."
     :why "Link edges apply via idempotent connect (UPSERT): kept/new pairs refresh, but pairs you dropped from the query are NEVER deleted, so the link count never falls. Reconcile once by severing the stale edge via the disconnect-<link-type> Action (api/mutate :disconnect-<type> {:id <edge-id>}); the corrected transform still prevents all FUTURE bad links."}
    {:rule "Don't bump `:semantic-version` to reprocess an object-type/link-type transform and expect stale rows gone."
     :why "Its truncate path only handles :dataset outputs; the object/link table keeps its old rows. Use force-snapshot on the producing dataset (then rebuild), or disconnect stale link edges."}]
   :see-also [:force-snapshot :incremental-sync :transform :link-type :run-import :check]}
  :action
  {:purpose "A named, parameterized, permissioned write operation on the ontology — the safe writeback primitive."
   :patterns
   ["Model a repeatable change as an Action (parameters + rules + submission criteria), not ad-hoc edits."
    "Gate the write with submission criteria — conditions on the current user and on parameters; an action only submits when ALL criteria pass."
    "When the change is beyond plain create/modify/delete rules — several linked objects at once, a value computed from other objects, or objects AND links together — back it with an edit Function: `{:op :apply-edit-function :function F}` as the action's ONLY rule, plus the `:edits` provenance it may touch. see :run-function."]
   :anti-patterns
   [{:rule "Don't stack contradictory Ontology rules in one action (create twice, or delete/modify before create)."
     :why "Rules compile to a single edit per object; unsupported combinations (create-twice, modify-before-create) are rejected."}]
   :see-also [:automate :run-function]}
  :automate
  {:purpose
   "Object-data-condition → submit-Action-effect: the in-platform writeback-on-trigger engine."
   :patterns
   ["Configure for BULK: a single-execution effect over the whole matched object set, with an action that accepts an object-set/array parameter; cap frequently-updating conditions with a time condition."
    "A per-object DECISION (classify, assign, route) is an edit Function with a `:use-llm` step plus an `:apply-action`, run by a Logic effect `{:function F :mode :propose}` — each proposed Action waits for accept/reject with the model's decision log beside it."
    "Scale limits: a SCHEDULED evaluation that runs per object (a Logic effect, or an Action effect that is not batched) over more than 1,000 matched objects is a runtime error — nothing runs, the run history says why; a manual `--do run` takes up to 5,000,000. A batched Action effect takes at most 1,000 objects per batch. Narrow `:when` rather than raising anything."]
   :anti-patterns
   [{:rule "Don't use an automation to set a property to a hardcoded or computed value."
     :why "That's just an Action — create the Action type and invoke it directly on the target object set; that is cheaper, and the caller decides when it runs."}]
   :see-also [:automations :action]}
  :schedule
  {:purpose "A time-trigger that fires a target-scoped build. It refreshes an external transform (IMAP/REST pull) ONLY when its :target IS that pull's landing dataset — a schedule targeting a downstream object-type re-maps existing raw data and does NOT re-pull."
   :patterns
   ["One schedule per pipeline: give it a descriptive name and enable retries (≈3, ≥1 min apart) plus abort-on-failure so problems surface fast."
    "To keep an external source fresh, point the schedule's :target at the pull's landing dataset (the external transform's output), not at a downstream object-type derived from it — otherwise the timer fires but the source is never re-pulled."]
   :anti-patterns [{:rule "Don't let two schedules build the same dataset (target)."
                    :why "Give each dataset exactly one schedule; two build it twice and wait on each other."}
                   {:rule "Don't assume `schedule <name> --do run` re-pulls the external system."
                    :why "It runs a target-scoped build; if the target is downstream of the raw landing dataset the external pull never executes. Verify by inspecting the run (`run <id>`): a real pull shows a non-trivial pull phase + rows on the landing transform. To force the pull use a full `run-import` (or `run-import --only <pull_transform>`)."}]
   :see-also [:schedules :run-import :incremental-sync]}
  :staging
  {:purpose "Schema changes execute on a persistent staging branch and are COMMITTED (merged onto the parent) as one structured commit — nothing is live until commit."
   :patterns
   ["Author → stage (install) → review/check/lint → commit. `context` shows the current staged count."]
   :see-also [:install :commit :check]}}}
